window.KANI_BOOTSTRAP = {"chapters":[{"id":"home","title":"Kani Manifesto","group":"Home","file":"home.md","description":"The ethos, people and practices of Kani Home. A shared framework for how we care, collaborate, make decisions and build programmes together."},{"id":"framework","title":"Programme framework","group":"Programme Framework","file":"framework.md","description":"How the ethos, pillars, people and platforms fit together."},{"id":"research","title":"Research, Residencies & Learning","group":"Programme Framework","file":"research.md","description":"Develop knowledge, creative practices, skills and new ways of working."},{"id":"creative-experiences","title":"Creative Experiences","group":"Programme Framework","file":"creative-experiences.md","description":"Bring people together through encounters with art, culture, ideas and making."},{"id":"commercial","title":"Commercial Experiences & Space Hire","group":"Programme Framework","file":"commercial.md","description":"Earn income through commissioned experiences, spaces and creative hospitality."},{"id":"outreach","title":"Participation, Outreach & Community Relationships","group":"Programme Framework","file":"outreach.md","description":"How participation and continuing relationships are built across the three pillars."},{"id":"programme-selection","title":"Programme Selection","group":"Programme Framework","file":"programme-selection.md","description":"How ideas enter, are reviewed, selected and developed."},{"id":"structure","title":"Overview","group":"How Kani Works","file":"structure.md","description":"How advice, governance, continuing work and programme delivery connect."},{"id":"structure-people","title":"People & Roles","group":"How Kani Works","file":"structure-people.md","description":"The Advisory Circle, Council, Working Team and Programme Teams."},{"id":"structure-appointments","title":"Appointments, Terms & Review","group":"How Kani Works","file":"structure-appointments.md","description":"How people enter, continue in and leave positions of responsibility."},{"id":"structure-decisions","title":"Decisions & Programme Authority","group":"How Kani Works","file":"structure-decisions.md","description":"Delegation, consensus, programme authority and escalation."},{"id":"structure-operations","title":"Operations & Resources","group":"How Kani Works","file":"structure-operations.md","description":"Partnerships, budgets, spaces, systems and shared services."},{"id":"structure-accountability","title":"Care, Concerns & Accountability","group":"How Kani Works","file":"structure-accountability.md","description":"Working disagreements, sensitive concerns and impartial review."},{"id":"heart-wellness","title":"Heart & Wellness","group":"Heart & Wellness","file":"heart-wellness.md","description":"Our shared foundation, with programmes for wellbeing, growth and becoming."},{"id":"infrastructure","title":"Shared infrastructure & capabilities","group":"Shared Infrastructure","file":"infrastructure.md","description":"Spaces, equipment and services supporting all programmes."},{"id":"platforms","title":"Platforms","group":"Platforms","file":"platforms.md","description":"Continuing structures that hold related programmes, projects and editions."},{"id":"kani-fest","title":"Kani Fest","group":"Platforms","file":"kani-fest.md","description":"A fresh curatorial proposition for each festival edition."},{"id":"publications","title":"Kani Publications","group":"Platforms","file":"publications.md","description":"A year-round publishing programme across the three pillars."},{"id":"marketplaces","title":"Curated Marketplaces","group":"Platforms","file":"marketplaces.md","description":"Artisans, makers, demonstrations and public exchange."},{"id":"new-initiatives","title":"Developing new initiatives","group":"Platforms","file":"new-initiatives.md","description":"Pilot, learn and establish what deserves to continue."},{"id":"learnings-feedback","title":"Learnings & Feedback","group":"Learnings & Feedback","file":"learnings-feedback.md","description":"Programme learning and an evolving record of feedback. Collection is ongoing."},{"id":"structure-archive","title":"Earlier Structures","group":"Review & Editing","file":"structure-archive.md","description":"Archived council, governance and staffing proposals."},{"id":"open-decisions","title":"Open decisions","group":"Review & Editing","file":"open-decisions.md","description":""},{"id":"version-history","title":"Version history","group":"Review & Editing","file":"version-history.md","description":"A record of how the thinking develops."},{"id":"document-plan","title":"Document review checklist","group":"Review & Editing","file":"document-plan.md","description":""},{"id":"editing","title":"How to edit and share","group":"Review & Editing","file":"editing.md","description":""},{"id":"structure-governance","title":"Governance (archived v0.11)","group":"Archive · Earlier Structures","file":"structure-governance.md","description":"The earlier separate Council and governance page.","archived":true},{"id":"structure-advisory","title":"Advisory Circle (archived v0.11)","group":"Archive · Earlier Structures","file":"structure-advisory.md","description":"The earlier separate Advisory Circle page.","archived":true},{"id":"structure-working-team","title":"Working Team (archived v0.11)","group":"Archive · Earlier Structures","file":"structure-working-team.md","description":"The earlier separate Working Team page.","archived":true},{"id":"structure-programme-teams","title":"Programme Teams (archived v0.11)","group":"Archive · Earlier Structures","file":"structure-programme-teams.md","description":"The earlier separate Programme Teams page.","archived":true},{"id":"structure-resolution","title":"Disagreements & Resolution (archived v0.11)","group":"Archive · Earlier Structures","file":"structure-resolution.md","description":"The earlier separate disagreements and resolution page.","archived":true},{"id":"entities","title":"Enabling entities (archived)","group":"Archive · Earlier Structures","file":"entities.md","description":"The organisations that enable the work.","archived":true},{"id":"council","title":"Council · Option 1 (archived)","group":"Archive · Earlier Structures","file":"council.md","description":"Seven advisory areas. One-year appointments, reviewed annually.","archived":true},{"id":"council-option-2","title":"Council · Option 2 (archived)","group":"Archive · Earlier Structures","file":"council-option-2.md","description":"A participatory council with advisors, curators, community and youth representatives.","archived":true},{"id":"council-option-3","title":"Council · Option 3 (archived)","group":"Archive · Earlier Structures","file":"council-option-3.md","description":"Shared stewardship, six advisors and a light Working Coordination Group.","archived":true},{"id":"decisions","title":"Decision rights (archived)","group":"Archive · Earlier Structures","file":"decisions.md","description":"Clear ownership for direction, creative decisions and delivery.","archived":true},{"id":"year-one","title":"Year 1 · Minimum team (archived)","group":"Archive · Earlier Structures","file":"year-one.md","description":"Three permanent hires, existing teams and defined specialist support.","archived":true},{"id":"full-structure","title":"Full structure (archived)","group":"Archive · Earlier Structures","file":"full-structure.md","description":"Dedicated capacity as programmes and recurring work grow.","archived":true},{"id":"growth","title":"How the team grows (archived)","group":"Archive · Earlier Structures","file":"growth.md","description":"Add people in response to sustained work and funding.","archived":true}],"settings":{"title":"Kani Manifesto","repository":"bhaumikdebanshu/KaniManifesto","branch":"main","version":"v0.13","stage":"Revised draft","updated":"12 November 2026","kicker":"An evolving manifesto","foundation":{"title":"Heart — our shared foundation","description":"Everything begins with heart and grace. We create space for people to discover their potential, nurture their practice and grow together."}},"content":{"home":"## Our ethos\n\nKani Home is a place for creative practice, research, learning and public exchange. It brings artists, designers, architects, researchers, craftspeople, musicians, ecologists, climate practitioners, heritage specialists, technologists and communities into conversation, creating space for both sustained enquiry and shared experiences.\n\nIts work is guided by:\n\n- **Heart & grace:** Bring care, openness and respect to everything we do. Support people in exploring who they are and who they wish to become, through reflection, creative practice and community.\n\n- **Community participation:** Develop programmes with local people, practitioners and knowledge holders.\n\n- **Experimentation:** Support exploration, making, testing and work whose outcomes are not predetermined.\n\n- **Ecological responsibility:** Engage with climate, water, materials and the relationships between people and their environments.\n\n- **Exchange across disciplines:** Connect art, design, craft, music, research and technology.\n\n- **Continuity of knowledge:** Document, publish and share work so that learning continues beyond an event or residency.\n\n- **Sustainable operation:** Develop earned income and external support that sustain the place, its people and its programme.\n\n## How we work together — a transdisciplinary practice\n\nKani brings different disciplines and forms of knowledge into shared enquiry. An artisan, technologist, researcher and local community might shape a project together, each influencing its questions, methods and outcomes. The three pillars give this work a structure while allowing projects to move between learning, public exchange and commissioned experiences.\n\n[Participation, Outreach & Community Relationships](#outreach) builds the continuing relationships that make this participation possible, from listening and co-developing ideas to supporting access and following up after a programme.\n\n[Heart](#heart-wellness) is both our way of being together and a space for growth. Through programmes such as Nurupa, we bring reflection, play, creative practice and mentorship together, helping people explore their potential and how they contribute to the world around them.\n\n## How Kani Works — a synopsis\n\nThree pillars guide what Kani does. Four connected groups shape how the work is supported, governed and delivered.\n\n```kani-structure\n- [Advisory Circle](#structure-people) | Offers specialist knowledge, mentoring, critical review and relationships. It advises without becoming a management or governing layer.\n- [Kani Council](#structure-people) | Holds the shared direction, agrees priorities and resource allocations, and remains accountable for how the organisation works.\n- [Kani Working Team](#structure-people) | Maintains year-round programmes, relationships, spaces and systems, and carries organisational continuity.\n- [Programme Teams](#structure-people) | Develop and deliver particular initiatives within an agreed brief, budget and period of work.\n```\n\nThe Advisory Circle strengthens the Council with knowledge and relationships; the Council gives the teams clear mandates and resources. The Working Team carries continuity, while Programme Teams gather around specific work. [Explore how Kani works](#structure) or [browse earlier proposals](#structure-archive).\n\n\n","framework":"Kani’s programme framework helps us understand what kinds of work Kani undertakes, how different activities connect and what deserves to continue over time.\n\nIt has three connected elements:\n\n- **Pillars** describe the purpose of the work.\n- **Programmes** are the activities and projects Kani develops.\n- **Platforms** are named, continuing structures that can hold multiple programmes, projects and editions.\n\n## What kinds of work does Kani undertake?\n\nKani’s work is organised through three pillars:\n\n| Pillar | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| **Research, Residencies & Learning** | Develop knowledge, creative practices, skills and new forms of enquiry. |\n| **Creative Experiences** | Bring people together through art, culture, ideas and making. |\n| **Commercial Experiences & Space Hire** | Generate income through commissioned experiences, hospitality and the use of Kani’s spaces. |\n\nThe pillars help us understand the primary purpose of a programme.\n\n## What holds this work together?\n\nHeart and grace form the foundation of all three pillars.\n\nCommunity participation, experimentation, ecological responsibility, transdisciplinary exchange, continuity of knowledge and sustainable operation shape how programmes are developed and delivered.\n\nThese principles apply whether the programme is a residency, festival, publication, gathering, workshop, marketplace, meal or commercial engagement.\n\n## What is the difference between a pillar, a programme and a platform?\n\n- **Pillars** describe why the work exists.\n- **Programmes** are the actual activities and projects being undertaken.\n- **Platforms** are recognisable, continuing structures through which related programmes can develop over time.\n\nA platform may hold multiple editions, projects, gatherings, collaborations and outputs.\n\nFor example, Creative Experiences is a pillar. Kanifest is a platform. A particular edition of Kanifest is a programme developed within that platform.\n\nPlatforms draw on Kani’s shared Working Team, spaces, infrastructure and programme-specific appointments.\n\nFormats such as exhibitions, workshops, residencies, meals, performances and publications do not automatically become platforms. They are ways in which programmes can take form.\n\n## Can a programme belong to more than one pillar?\n\nYes. Transdisciplinary programmes can move between research, public exchange and commissioned or income-generating activity.\n\nA residency might bring an artisan, technologist and local knowledge holder together to explore a question. Their research could become an exhibition, workshop or publication. An institution might later commission a related learning experience.\n\nThe same enquiry can move across the pillars without losing its original purpose.\n\n## Can a platform work across more than one pillar?\n\nYes. A platform can hold different kinds of programmes.\n\nKani Publications may document research, produce an artist publication and undertake a commissioned editorial project. Kani Canteen and Food Lab may research local ingredients, host community meals and develop paid food experiences.\n\nThis movement across the pillars is central to Kani’s transdisciplinary approach.\n\n## What are the current platforms?\n\n| Platform | What it holds |\n|---|---|\n| **Kanifest** | An interdisciplinary festival developed through distinct editions, curatorial propositions and community participation. |\n| **Kani Publications** | Editorial projects, research outputs, field notes, books and other forms of documented knowledge. |\n| **Curated Marketplaces** | Recurring encounters connecting artisans, makers, demonstrations, workshops and cultural programming. |\n| **Sunset Baithaks** | Intimate sunset gatherings around music, poetry, performance, conversation and collective listening. |\n| **Kani Canteen and Food Lab** | A space for food experimentation, shared meals, local ingredients, culinary knowledge and feeding the community. |\n\nOther platforms may emerge as Kani’s work develops. Not every workshop, exhibition, residency, gathering or event needs to become a platform.\n\n## How does Kani decide what to programme?\n\nA proposed programme should have:\n\n- A clear relationship to Kani’s ethos and one or more pillars\n- Relevance to a community, audience or field of enquiry\n- A named creative owner and delivery owner\n- Realistic time, resources and funding\n- An approach to participation, access and care\n- A plan for documentation, reflection or continued learning\n\nProgrammes can begin as small experiments. They may be repeated, revised, expanded or allowed to conclude. See [Programme Selection](#programme-selection) for the complete process.\n\n## When does a programme become a platform?\n\nA programme can develop into a platform when it has:\n\n- A clear purpose and recognisable identity\n- A reason to continue beyond a single event or project\n- The capacity to hold multiple programmes, editions or collaborations\n- A named owner\n- A realistic resource and funding model\n- A way to carry relationships, documentation and learning forward\n\nBecoming a platform is not automatically a measure of success. Some of Kani’s most meaningful programmes may remain temporary, experimental or specific to a particular place and moment.\n","research":"Research, Residencies & Learning gives people time, space, relationships and resources through which to investigate questions, develop practices and share knowledge.\n\nWork may begin with artistic practice, ecology, craft, technology, lived experience or local knowledge. It may result in publications, exhibitions, prototypes, performances, gatherings or new forms of learning.\n\n## How does the work happen?\n\n| Pathway | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| **Research** | Investigate questions through artistic, ecological, technological, material and community knowledge. |\n| **Residencies** | Give artists, researchers, makers and other practitioners supported time to develop their work in relation to Kani’s place and communities. |\n| **Learning** | Exchange knowledge through mentorship, workshops, field schools, demonstrations and collaborative practice. |\n\nA programme may combine all three pathways. A residency might begin with research, involve a local learning exchange and conclude with an exhibition or publication.\n\n## What can be explored?\n\nAreas of enquiry may include:\n\n- Water, climate, local ecologies and speculative futures\n- Craft, materials, making and sustainable production\n- Creative technology and its cultural and social implications\n- Oral histories, living knowledge and community-led archives\n- Reflective practice, wellbeing and personal growth\n- Transdisciplinary collaborations that connect different forms of knowledge\n\nThese are areas of enquiry, not permanent departments. Appropriate practitioners, knowledge holders and specialists are invited according to the needs of each programme.\n\nHeart programmes such as Nurupa may use residential, mentored or hybrid formats to connect self-enquiry, creative practice and community learning. See [Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness).\n\n## What should each programme put in place?\n\nEvery research, residency or learning programme should make clear:\n\n- The central question or field of enquiry\n- Who is participating and who is shaping the work\n- The time, space, mentorship, equipment and financial support available\n- How local and specialist knowledge will be credited and compensated\n- What access, care and consent are required\n- What may be documented, shared or kept private\n- What should remain after the programme\n\nThe selection of individual artists, residents, fellows or contributors follows the approved programme’s own brief and criteria. See [Programme Selection](#programme-selection).\n\n## What can the work produce?\n\nThe work may produce an exhibition, performance, publication, prototype, archive, workshop, public conversation or further research. Not every programme needs a public presentation or a finished object.\n\nKani Publications and other platforms may carry research into public life, but the enquiry should not be forced into an outcome that does not serve the work.\n\n## How do we know the work is meaningful?\n\nWe ask whether the programme:\n\n- Creates space for a significant question to be explored\n- Supports genuine exchange between disciplines and forms of knowledge\n- Treats participants and knowledge holders fairly\n- Deepens understanding of a place, practice or subject\n- Produces learning that can be carried forward\n- Opens a useful next step, even when the original enquiry remains unresolved\n","creative-experiences":"Creative Experiences bring people into meaningful encounters with art, culture, ideas, food and making.\n\nPeople may observe, listen, participate, contribute or create together. The emphasis is not only on what is presented, but also on the relationships and experiences that form around it.\n\n## How can people encounter the work?\n\n| Mode | Possible forms |\n|---|---|\n| **Gather** | Festivals, baithaks, conversations, readings and community meals |\n| **Experience** | Exhibitions, performances, screenings, installations and open studios |\n| **Make** | Workshops, demonstrations and participatory activities |\n| **Exchange** | Markets, walks, talks and encounters across disciplines |\n| **Share** | Publications, presentations and public reflections on work in progress |\n\nThese are formats through which programmes can take form. They do not automatically become platforms.\n\nPlatforms such as Kanifest, Sunset Baithaks, Curated Marketplaces and Kani Publications provide continuing structures within which different creative programmes may develop. See [Platforms](#platforms).\n\n## How do people participate?\n\nEvery programme should consider whether people are being invited to:\n\n- Witness or listen\n- Learn or make\n- Contribute knowledge or experience\n- Help shape the programme\n- Perform, exhibit or present\n- Return and build a longer relationship with Kani\n\nParticipation should be meaningful rather than symbolic. When a programme draws from a community, place or knowledge tradition, relevant people should be involved in shaping how it is represented.\n\n## What makes an experience distinctly Kani?\n\nA Creative Experience should:\n\n- Connect with Kani’s ethos and one or more pillars\n- Create room for curiosity, care and exchange\n- Bring disciplines, practices or communities into conversation\n- Consider language, physical access, affordability and cultural context\n- Respect the people whose work and knowledge are being presented\n- Leave space for reflection, documentation or continued relationships\n\nNot every experience needs to be large. An intimate conversation, shared meal or work-in-progress presentation can be as meaningful as a festival or exhibition.\n\n## Does charging make a programme commercial?\n\nNo. A ticketed event, workshop or marketplace may remain within Creative Experiences when its primary purpose is cultural, educational or community-facing.\n\nA programme belongs primarily to the commercial pillar when a client is purchasing a space, service or commissioned outcome. Its purpose—not only its source of income—determines its pillar.\n\n## How do we know the experience is meaningful?\n\nWe ask whether:\n\n- The intended people were able to participate\n- The form supported the idea rather than overwhelming it\n- Participants were treated with care and respect\n- Genuine exchange took place\n- The experience created new understanding, relationships or possibilities\n- Learning from the programme can improve what Kani does next\n\n","commercial":"Commercial Experiences & Space Hire generate earned income through Kani’s spaces, hospitality and creative capabilities.\n\nThis work helps sustain Kani’s people, infrastructure and public-interest programmes while remaining consistent with its ethos.\n\n## How does Kani generate earned income?\n\n| Model | What it may include |\n|---|---|\n| **Use Kani’s spaces** | Gallery hire, screenings, presentations, meetings, private events and short-term workspace |\n| **Stay and gather at Kani** | Retreats, offsites, guest stays, hospitality and residential team programmes |\n| **Commission Kani’s practice** | Creative-learning experiences, publications, workshops and tailored cultural projects developed by Kani |\n| **Design a programme for your space** | Curatorial concepts, programme strategy, contributor selection, partnerships, schedules and delivery frameworks developed for another space or institution |\n\nKani may design a complete programme for a cultural space, school, institution, workplace, hospitality venue or other public-facing environment. This could involve understanding the place and its audiences, developing a curatorial direction, identifying artists or practitioners, building partnerships and creating a plan for delivery.\n\nThe programme may be delivered by the commissioning organisation, produced jointly with Kani or managed by Kani as an additional service.\n\nA commercial engagement may also combine programme design with the use of Kani’s spaces, accommodation, food, facilitation or creative production.\n\n## What makes an engagement commercial?\n\nAn engagement is primarily commercial when a client purchases:\n\n- The use of a space or facility\n- Hospitality or accommodation\n- A facilitated experience\n- A commissioned programme, publication or creative outcome\n- Curatorial or programme-design expertise for another space\n\nCharging for a public workshop, festival or cultural programme does not automatically make it commercial.\n\nSimilarly, a partner-funded programme may remain a research or creative programme when its primary purpose is public, educational or community-facing.\n\n## What principles guide commercial work?\n\nCommercial engagements should:\n\n- Recover the full cost of delivery\n- Contribute to Kani’s shared infrastructure and long-term sustainability\n- Pay people fairly and account for staff time\n- Remain consistent with Kani’s ecological, cultural and community commitments\n- Avoid regularly displacing Kani’s own programmes\n- Protect appropriate curatorial and editorial independence\n- Use community relationships and cultural knowledge responsibly\n- Make clear what Kani is providing and what the client is purchasing\n\nWhen Kani designs or curates a programme for another organisation, the agreement should clarify who holds final curatorial authority, who selects and contracts contributors, and who is responsible for production and delivery.\n\nCommercial activity should support Kani’s purpose rather than gradually redefine it.\n\n## What must be agreed before proceeding?\n\nEvery commercial engagement requires:\n\n- A defined scope and intended outcome\n- A named relationship owner and delivery owner\n- A realistic budget, price and payment schedule\n- An account of staff, space, equipment and hospitality costs\n- A written agreement covering responsibilities, permissions and cancellations\n- A confirmed place in the shared calendar\n- A review of operational, financial and reputational risks\n- Clarity about documentation, publicity and the use of Kani’s name\n\nFor programmes developed for another space, the agreement should also define:\n\n- The curatorial brief and intended audience\n- Who approves the programme\n- Who appoints and pays participating practitioners\n- Whether Kani is advising, co-producing or delivering the programme\n- How the programme and Kani’s role will be credited\n\nSee [Operations & Resources](#structure-operations) for delivery responsibilities and institutional partnerships.\n\n## How do we know the work is successful?\n\nWe ask whether the engagement:\n\n- Created appropriate value for the client or partner\n- Paid for its complete use of Kani’s resources\n- Contributed financially to Kani’s continuity\n- Was delivered without unreasonable pressure on people or infrastructure\n- Strengthened rather than compromised Kani’s reputation and relationships\n- Created a worthwhile reason to work together again\n","outreach":"Participation, Outreach & Community Relationships is a practice that runs across all three pillars and every Kani programme. It is not a separate pillar.\n\nCommunications tells people what is happening. Outreach helps people enter the work. Community engagement creates opportunities for people to shape it. Relationship-building allows the connection to continue beyond a single event.\n\n## What are the different functions?\n\n| Function | Primary responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| **Community engagement** | Building long-term relationships with local communities, practitioners and knowledge holders |\n| **Programme outreach** | Bringing relevant participants and contributors into a particular programme |\n| **Communications** | Sharing clear public information and helping audiences discover Kani’s work |\n| **Institutional partnerships** | Developing relationships with schools, cultural organisations, funders and other collaborators |\n\nThese functions support one another, but they are not interchangeable. Public visibility is not the same as community participation, and an institutional partnership does not replace direct relationships with the people affected by a programme.\n\n## How does a relationship develop?\n\n| Stage | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| **Listen** | Understand existing interests, concerns, knowledge and relationships before defining the programme |\n| **Invite and shape** | Identify who should participate and who should help influence the work |\n| **Enable participation** | Address barriers involving language, cost, travel, timing, access, confidence or care |\n| **Stay connected** | Maintain communication during and after the programme |\n| **Respond and learn** | Record what was heard, what changed and who will follow up |\n\nOutreach begins during programme development—not after the programme has already been designed.\n\n## What must every programme do?\n\nEvery programme should identify:\n\n- Who it hopes to reach\n- Who should help shape the work\n- Who holds each relationship\n- What barriers may prevent participation\n- What access or participation support is required\n- What commitments are being made\n- How feedback will be received and answered\n- How relationships will continue or be respectfully concluded\n\nRelevant outreach costs should be included in the programme budget. These may include visits, translation, travel, access support, facilitation or additional coordination.\n\n## Who carries the work?\n\nThe person holding **Community & Care** maintains continuity in Kani’s local and community relationships.\n\nProgramme leads remain responsible for the invitations, promises and relationships created through their programmes.\n\n**Design & Communications** makes public information understandable, accessible and visually coherent.\n\nThe **Social Media & Audience Coordinator** maintains Kani’s social media calendar and public-facing digital presence. They work with programme leads and Design & Communications to publish accurate and timely content, coordinate programme assets with appropriate consent, respond to public enquiries and review audience engagement.\n\nThis role supports discovery and public conversation; it does not replace the longer-term community relationships held through Community & Care or the invitations and commitments held by programme leads.\n\nThe **Partnerships lead** maintains institutional, educational, funding and strategic relationships.\n\nBusy or geographically distributed programmes may appoint additional outreach capacity. Volunteer coordination remains a separate delivery responsibility within Kanifest and other large programmes.\n\n## How are relationships recorded responsibly?\n\nKani maintains a shared internal record of interests, commitments and follow-ups. Personal contact details are stored only with appropriate consent and access.\n\nThe record should help the team remember relationships; it should not reduce people to an audience or marketing list.\n\n## How do we know outreach is meaningful?\n\nWe ask whether:\n\n- Relevant people were involved early enough to influence the work\n- Participation extended beyond the people already closest to Kani\n- Barriers were recognised and addressed\n- Commitments were followed through\n- Feedback resulted in a response or visible change\n- Relationships can continue beyond a single programme\n\nSee [People & Roles](#structure-people) for continuing responsibility and [Learnings & Feedback](#learnings-feedback) for how programme learning is carried forward.\n","programme-selection":"This process helps Kani choose what to develop, support, host or undertake with a partner. It leaves room for experimentation, community proposals and unexpected collaborations while making decisions understandable and accountable.\n\nNot every idea needs to begin as a complete proposal, and not every opportunity needs to become a competitive application.\n\n## What does this process select?\n\nIt selects the programmes Kani will commit its name, time, relationships, spaces or resources to.\n\nThese may include:\n\n- Research projects, residencies and learning programmes\n- Exhibitions, workshops, gatherings and performances\n- Publications, meals and public experiences\n- Commercial bookings and commissioned experiences\n- Programmes designed or curated for another space\n- Collaborations developed with communities, institutions or funders\n\nThe selection of individual artists, residents, fellows, facilitators, contributors or vendors happens after the programme itself has been approved.\n\n## How can an idea enter?\n\nAn idea may come from:\n\n- The Working Team or a Programme Team\n- Community members and local practitioners\n- The Advisory Circle or Council\n- An artist, researcher, institution or potential partner\n- A client or commissioning organisation\n- An open call, invitation or commissioned opportunity\n- Learning from an earlier programme\n\nNo route gives an idea automatic priority. Invited, commissioned and partner-supported proposals still require an appropriate review.\n\n## What kind of proposal is it?\n\nBefore reviewing an idea, Kani identifies:\n\n- Its primary pillar\n- Any secondary pillar it connects to\n- Whether Kani is the initiator, host, partner, commissioned curator or delivery team\n- Whether it will happen at Kani Home, elsewhere or across both\n- Whether it is a one-time programme or sits within an existing platform\n\nA programme may connect to more than one pillar, but naming its primary purpose helps Kani decide how it should be reviewed.\n\nA programme does not need to be identified as a new platform at this stage. Platforms emerge only when there is a clear reason and capacity for work to continue over time.\n\n## What information is needed?\n\nAn idea begins with a short programme note that explains:\n\n- The central idea, enquiry or intended experience\n- Its relationship to Kani’s ethos and pillars\n- Kani’s proposed role\n- Who it is for and who should help shape it\n- The proposed creative owner and delivery owner\n- Likely activities, outputs and timeline\n- Where the programme will take place\n- Space, people, equipment and partnership needs\n- An initial budget and possible source of funds\n- Participation, access, care and ecological considerations\n- Who will hold the relevant relationships\n- What should be documented, learned or carried forward\n\nFor commissioned work, the note should also identify the client, what they are purchasing and whether Kani is advising, co-producing or delivering the programme.\n\nEarly ideas do not need a complete proposal. The note should provide enough information to decide whether further development is worthwhile.\n\n## Who reviews an idea?\n\nThe **Programme Coordinator** holds the process and brings together the people required for the review.\n\n- The relevant programme or creative lead considers purpose, quality and programme fit.\n- The **Operations Coordinator** checks space, timing, staffing, equipment and technical capacity.\n- **Community & Care** contributes when participation, access or continuing relationships are important.\n- **Partnerships and Accounts** contribute where funding, contracts, pricing or reporting are involved.\n- Relevant community members, practitioners or knowledge holders are included when the work affects, represents or draws knowledge from them.\n- Advisors may contribute specialist knowledge, critical review or relationships.\n- The Council considers proposals only when they change Kani’s direction, require a major allocation or create commitments beyond the Working Team’s mandate.\n\nThe people who develop an idea may join the discussion, but the decision and its reasons should not rest with one person alone.\n\n## What questions apply to every proposal?\n\n| Question | What to consider |\n|---|---|\n| **Does it belong at Kani?** | Its relationship to Kani’s ethos, pillars, place and longer direction |\n| **Who finds it meaningful?** | Its relevance to participants, communities, practitioners, audiences or a field of enquiry |\n| **Who should shape it?** | Whose knowledge, experience or participation is needed during development |\n| **Does it invite genuine exchange?** | Its transdisciplinary possibilities and the quality of participation it creates |\n| **Can it be cared for well?** | Access, consent, wellbeing, fair compensation, representation and realistic workloads |\n| **Can it be delivered?** | Time, people, space, equipment, permissions, partnerships and operational complexity |\n| **Can it be sustained financially?** | Budget, funding route, earned income where appropriate and the complete cost to Kani |\n| **What will remain?** | Learning, relationships, documentation, public value or a reason to continue |\n\nThese questions support judgement; they are not a mechanical score. A small local experiment may be more valuable than a larger, better-funded proposal.\n\n## What additional questions depend on the kind of work?\n\n| Pathway | Additional questions |\n|---|---|\n| **Research, Residencies & Learning** | Is there a meaningful enquiry? What time, mentorship, space and financial support are required? How will knowledge be credited? Is a public outcome appropriate? |\n| **Creative Experiences** | How will people encounter or shape the work? Is it accessible? Does the proposed format serve the idea? What kind of relationship should continue afterwards? |\n| **Commercial Experiences & Space Hire** | What is the client purchasing? Does the price recover the full cost? Will the work contribute to Kani’s sustainability without compromising its ethos or displacing its own programmes? |\n| **Programme design for another space** | Is Kani advising, co-producing or delivering? Who holds curatorial authority? Who approves the programme? Who selects, contracts and pays contributors? |\n| **Community or partner-supported work** | Were relevant people involved early enough? Are community and institutional relationships being treated distinctly? Who is responsible for commitments and follow-up? |\n\nA programme that moves across pillars may need questions from more than one pathway.\n\n## How are people selected?\n\nProgramme approval comes before the selection of individual participants or contributors.\n\nEach approved programme defines:\n\n- The people or skills it needs\n- Whether selection will happen through an open call, invitation, nomination, commission or procurement process\n- The criteria that will be used\n- Who will make the decision\n- What payment, support and working conditions are available\n- How conflicts of interest will be handled\n- How applicants or invitees will be informed\n\nNot every opportunity requires an open call. A direct invitation may be appropriate when a programme requires a particular practice, relationship or form of knowledge. The reason for the invitation should still be recorded and understandable.\n\n## What decisions can be made?\n\n| Decision | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Develop** | The idea is promising and receives time to prepare a fuller brief |\n| **Pilot** | Test the idea at a limited scale before making a larger commitment |\n| **Proceed** | Confirm the brief, owners, budget, authority and review point |\n| **Revise** | Return with specific changes to the scope, method, relationships or resources |\n| **Hold** | Keep the idea for a more suitable time, capacity or funding opportunity |\n| **Decline** | Do not proceed, with a short and respectful reason |\n\nEvery decision identifies who will communicate the outcome and what happens next.\n\n## How are fairness and accountability protected?\n\nAnyone involved in a decision declares personal, financial or professional conflicts. A conflicted person may provide factual context but does not make the final decision.\n\nSelection criteria and decision-makers are stated in advance for open calls and paid opportunities. Applicants receive clear information about the process, timeline, available support and expected commitments. Sensitive personal information remains private.\n\nKani keeps a short internal decision record containing:\n\n- The proposal\n- The people consulted\n- The decision and its reasons\n- Any conditions that must be met\n- The people responsible for the next steps\n- The agreed review point\n\nPublic communication may use an appropriate summary.\n\n## What happens after selection?\n\nBefore work begins, every programme receives an agreed brief, named owners, budget, authority, schedule and review point. Selection is not permission to exceed these boundaries.\n\nDepending on the kind of work, the programme may also require:\n\n- A participant-support and mentorship plan\n- A participation, access and outreach plan\n- A documentation, consent and knowledge-crediting agreement\n- A commercial scope, full-cost budget and client contract\n- An agreement defining curatorial authority and production responsibility\n- A process for selecting and contracting contributors\n\nAfter delivery, the team reviews the programme’s value, participation, workload, finances, relationships and learning.\n\nThe programme may conclude, be repeated, be revised or—when there is a clear purpose, identity and capacity to continue—develop into a platform.\n","structure":"Kani Home brings together creative practice, learning, community and enterprise. Its structure should help people take responsibility, work together and care for the place without concentrating every decision in one person.\n\n## What is the structure?\n\nKani works through four connected parts:\n\n| Part | Main responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| **Advisory Circle** | Contributes specialist knowledge, mentoring, critical review and relationships without becoming another management layer |\n| **Kani Council** | Holds Kani’s shared direction, agrees priorities and overall resource allocations, and remains accountable for how the organisation works |\n| **Working Team** | Maintains the year-round programmes, relationships, spaces and systems that make the work possible |\n| **Programme Teams** | Develop and deliver particular programmes within an agreed purpose, budget, period and mandate |\n\nThe Advisory Circle strengthens the work with knowledge and relationships. The Council provides direction and clear mandates. The Working Team maintains continuity. Programme Teams gather around particular work and hold authority within their approved brief.\n\nThe Advisory Circle appears first because it brings a wide field of knowledge into Kani. It does not sit above the Council in authority. Advisors contribute; the Council remains responsible for organisational decisions.\n\n## How does a programme move through the structure?\n\n1. An idea enters through the Working Team, a community member, advisor, institution, practitioner, client or earlier programme.\n2. The Programme Coordinator helps identify its primary purpose, Kani’s role and the people needed to review it.\n3. A temporary review group considers its purpose, participation, capacity, finances and pathway-specific requirements.\n4. The idea may be developed, piloted, approved, revised, held or declined.\n5. Once approved, it receives a brief, budget, authority, review point and the Programme Team it needs.\n6. After delivery, its value, relationships, finances, workload and learning are reviewed.\n\nThe Council becomes involved only when a proposal changes Kani’s direction, requires a major allocation, establishes a new platform or creates commitments beyond the Working Team’s mandate.\n\nSee [Programme Selection](#programme-selection) for the complete process.\n\n## Where does authority sit?\n\nAuthority follows responsibility.\n\n- A curator or creative lead makes content decisions within an agreed brief.\n- A producer or delivery lead organises delivery within agreed resources.\n- Continuing Working Team members make routine decisions within their mandates.\n- Appointment groups select people for defined roles.\n- The Council decides matters affecting Kani as a whole.\n- Authorised people and enabling entities complete contracts, payments and other reserved commitments.\n\nAdvisors, founders, partners and funders use these routes rather than giving parallel instructions to team members.\n\nEvery programme and continuing function has a named owner. Shared governance should make responsibility easier to understand, not make ownership disappear.\n\n## What does the starting structure look like?\n\nThe proposed starting structure is:\n\n- An Advisory Circle covering six areas of contribution\n- A seven-member Council, with the option to expand to nine when a clear representation gap is identified\n- Four continuing Working Team roles\n- Named paid capacity for partnerships and community care\n- Existing Accounts and Hospitality teams\n- Funded Programme Teams appointed according to need\n\nResponsibilities may be combined when skills, time and compensation allow. Additional work should not be assumed to happen informally or without support.\n\nCouncil seats are governance responsibilities rather than staff positions. Advisors and Council members may contribute without holding paid employment, but honoraria, travel and access support should be provided where possible.\n\nThe structure should be reviewed after six months. New continuing roles or Council seats are added only when there is a clear need and sufficient capacity to support them.\n\n## What holds the structure together?\n\n**Whatever we do, we do with heart and grace.**\n\nThis shapes how people are welcomed, how authority is exercised, how work is distributed, how disagreements are handled and how contributions are recognised.\n\nCare is a shared responsibility. It includes realistic workloads, fair compensation, accessible participation, respect for consent and a safe route for raising concerns.\n\nSee [Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness).\n\n## Where can I find more detail?\n\n| Page | What it covers |\n|---|---|\n| [People & Roles](#structure-people) | Advisory Circle, Council, Working Team and Programme Teams |\n| [Appointments, Terms & Review](#structure-appointments) | How people enter, continue in and leave positions of responsibility |\n| [Decisions & Programme Authority](#structure-decisions) | Delegation, consensus, programme approval and escalation |\n| [Operations & Resources](#structure-operations) | Partnerships, budgets, spaces, systems and shared services |\n| [Care, Concerns & Accountability](#structure-accountability) | Working disagreements, confidential concerns and impartial review |\n","structure-people":"## What is the Kani Council?\n\nThe Kani Council holds responsibility for Kani’s shared direction and accountability. It connects people doing the work with community perspectives and the legal, financial and institutional stewardship of the place.\n\nCouncil membership begins with seven seats and may expand to nine when a review identifies a clear gap in representation.\n\n### Council membership\n\n| Members | Seats | Contribution |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Working Team representatives | 2 | Programme knowledge, operations, capacity and everyday working conditions |\n| Community representatives | 2 | Local knowledge, participation, access and community priorities |\n| Youth representative | 1 | Young people’s perspectives on learning, participation and Kani’s future |\n| Founding and enabling-entity representatives | 2 | Institutional continuity, legal and financial responsibilities, and stewardship of the place |\n| Independent public-interest or practitioner representatives | 0–2 | Additional perspectives where a specific gap in representation or accountability has been identified |\n| **Total** | **7–9** | Members share responsibility for Kani as a whole |\n\nAdditional seats should not be created simply to include more expertise. Expertise can enter through the Advisory Circle. A Council seat is appropriate when someone needs to share continuing governance responsibility.\n\nCouncil seats are not additional staff positions or honorary titles. Members must be able to prepare for decisions, represent perspectives beyond themselves, declare conflicts and remain accountable for their participation.\n\nCouncil roles may be unpaid, but participation time, honoraria, travel and access support should be agreed wherever possible.\n\n### What does the Council do?\n\nThe Council:\n\n- Protects Kani’s purpose and ethos\n- Agrees annual priorities and overall budget allocations\n- Reviews the balance of work across the three pillars\n- Approves major partnerships, new platforms and long-term commitments\n- Reviews programme outcomes, finances and working conditions\n- Establishes clear responsibilities and decision boundaries\n- Oversees fair appointment and review processes\n- Reviews whether Kani’s governance and selection processes are working\n\nThe Council does not select every artist, approve every purchase or issue routine instructions to the Working Team.\n\nOne member serves as **Council Convenor** for a limited term. The Convenor gathers agenda items, circulates information and follows up on decisions. Facilitation may rotate. The Convenor has no casting vote or unilateral authority.\n\n## What is the Advisory Circle?\n\nThe Advisory Circle brings experience, reflection and relationships into Kani without becoming another authority layer.\n\nIt can be broader and more flexible than the Council because advisors do not carry governance votes or organisational decision-making authority.\n\n| Area | Contribution |\n|---|---|\n| **Arts, Culture & Publishing** | Creative practice, curatorial mentoring, publishing and cultural relationships |\n| **Research & Learning** | Residencies, fellowships, research collaborations and learning methods |\n| **Systems & Organisational Development** | Governance, documentation, team structures and organisational learning |\n| **Technology & Digital Practice** | Creative technology, digital infrastructure, archives and technical capability |\n| **Business & Enterprise** | Revenue models, pricing, commercial experiences, hospitality and sustainability |\n| **Institutional Partnerships & Fundraising** | Institutional networks, grants, CSR, sponsorship and resource mobilisation |\n\nThese are six areas of contribution, not necessarily six separate people. One area may include more than one advisor, and one advisor may contribute across connected areas.\n\nAdditional specialists may be invited for a particular question without creating new Council seats or permanent appointments.\n\nAdvisors may:\n\n- Mentor members of the Working Team and Programme Teams\n- Review proposals when their expertise is relevant\n- Strengthen practitioner, institutional, funding and industry relationships\n- Support organisational learning and capability\n- Contribute an independent view to periodic reviews\n\nAdvisors do not:\n\n- Hold a governance vote through their advisory appointment\n- Manage staff\n- Approve routine spending\n- Direct Programme Teams\n- Automatically join selection panels\n- Make commitments on behalf of Kani\n\nAn introduction or recommendation becomes work only when a named Working Team member accepts responsibility for it. If an advisor recommends a person or organisation being considered, the relationship is declared.\n\n## What is the Working Team?\n\nThe Working Team maintains continuity across Kani’s programmes and keeps the place functioning throughout the year.\n\n### Continuing roles\n\n| Role | Main responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| **Programme Coordinator** | Maintains the programme pipeline, convenes appropriate reviews, connects initiatives, supports programme leads and carries learning between programmes |\n| **Operations Coordinator** | Maintains the shared calendar, resource plan, venue readiness, working systems and coordination with Accounts, Hospitality and technical support |\n| **Design & Communications** | Maintains Kani’s visual identity, website, printed and digital communication, confirmed programme information and documentation standards |\n| **Social Media & Audience Coordinator** | Maintains the social media calendar and public-facing digital presence; coordinates programme campaigns, audience enquiries, content assets, consent and engagement review |\n\nThe Programme Coordinator holds the selection process but does not approve every programme alone.\n\n### Allocated or retained responsibilities\n\n| Responsibility | Starting arrangement |\n|---|---|\n| **Partnerships, Funding & Commercial Development** | A named part-time or retained person maintains institutional and client relationships, develops funding and commercial opportunities, supports proposals and holds reporting commitments |\n| **Community & Care** | Allocated paid time within a suitable role, or part-time support, for community relationships, participation, access, feedback and practical care |\n\nThese responsibilities require named capacity even when they are not separate full-time positions.\n\nThe Working Team meets regularly to review:\n\n- Programme proposals and upcoming commitments\n- Capacity and workload\n- Space, equipment and scheduling conflicts\n- Partnerships, funding and commercial enquiries\n- Community relationships and access requirements\n- Public communication, audience activity and upcoming campaigns\n- Decisions that may require Council involvement\n\nCoordination does not make one person the head of the team. Each role has a written mandate and a named support and review contact.\n\n## What are Programme Teams?\n\nProgramme Teams are temporary or continuing groups formed to develop and deliver approved programmes.\n\nA Programme Team is created only after the programme has:\n\n- Been reviewed and approved\n- Received a clear brief\n- Identified its creative and delivery authority\n- Secured an appropriate budget and resource plan\n- Established its participation, care and review requirements\n\nA small programme may have one person holding both creative and delivery responsibility. Larger programmes should separate these roles.\n\nPossible appointments include:\n\n- Curator or creative lead\n- Producer or delivery lead\n- Researcher, mentor or facilitator\n- Community or participant liaison\n- Editor or publication lead\n- Technical and production specialists\n- Artists, residents, fellows or contributors\n- Volunteer coordinator for programmes using volunteer teams\n\nProgramme appointments do not automatically create Council seats or authority beyond the approved brief.\n\nAt the end of a programme, its team hands over relevant relationships, records, financial information, documentation and learning to the appropriate continuing Working Team member.\n\n## How do the different parts relate?\n\n- The **Advisory Circle** contributes expertise and relationships.\n- The **Council** holds direction and accountability.\n- The **Working Team** maintains continuity and shared systems.\n- **Programme Teams** hold creative and delivery authority for particular work.\n\nA person may hold more than one role, but the responsibilities, decision authority and conflicts associated with each role must remain clear.\n","structure-appointments":"This page applies to organisational, advisory, creative and operational appointments. Selection of programme participants is covered under [Programme Selection](#programme-selection).\n\nAppointments are where people receive responsibility and authority. The process should therefore be understandable, proportionate and protected from control by any one person.\n\n## What principles guide appointments?\n\nAppointments should be based on:\n\n- A clear need and written role\n- Relevant capability, experience and relationships\n- Alignment with Kani’s ethos\n- Fair compensation and realistic time expectations\n- Appropriate representation and access\n- Declared conflicts of interest\n- Defined authority and accountability\n- A term or review point\n- A planned handover\n\nAppointment to an advisory, programme or operational role does not automatically create a Council seat or authority beyond the written mandate.\n\n## What appointment routes can Kani use?\n\n| Route | When it may be appropriate |\n|---|---|\n| **Representative selection** | Council seats representing the Working Team, communities or young people |\n| **Open application** | Continuing paid roles or opportunities where wider access and comparison are valuable |\n| **Open nomination** | Council, advisory or specialist roles where relevant groups can put people forward |\n| **Invitation** | A role requiring a particular practice, relationship, experience or form of knowledge |\n| **Commission** | A defined creative, curatorial or specialist contribution |\n| **Internal allocation** | A bounded responsibility assigned within an existing paid role |\n| **Procurement** | Vendors or professional services selected against scope, cost and capability |\n\nNot every role requires an open call. The chosen route and the reason for it should be stated before selection begins.\n\n## Who appoints whom?\n\n| Appointment | Decision route |\n|---|---|\n| Working Team representatives to the Council | Selected by the Working Team |\n| Community representatives | Selected through an accessible process developed with relevant local participants |\n| Youth representative | Selected through a process that meaningfully involves young people |\n| Founding and enabling-entity representatives | Named through a transparent process that states the authority they carry |\n| Independent public-interest or practitioner representatives | Selected through open nomination or expression of interest by a non-conflicted group including Council, Working Team and community representation |\n| Council Convenor | Chosen by the Council from among its members for a limited term |\n| Advisory Circle members | Nominated or invited for defined expertise and confirmed by the Council after appropriate consultation |\n| Continuing Working Team roles | Selected by a non-conflicted appointment group established by the Council, with relevant team and specialist input |\n| Programme leads and curators | Selected through the route named in the approved programme brief |\n| Programme contributors and participants | Selected through the approved programme’s people-selection process |\n| Temporary specialists and vendors | Appointed by the authorised programme or operational lead within the approved budget and procurement process |\n\nAn enabling entity may need to issue an employment contract or sign an agreement. That legal action should follow the agreed appointment decision rather than silently replace it.\n\n## What happens before selection?\n\nEvery appointment begins with a short role brief stating:\n\n- Why the role exists\n- Responsibilities and expected contribution\n- Decision authority and limits\n- Reporting, support and review relationships\n- Time commitment and working period\n- Compensation, honorarium or fee\n- Required experience or capability\n- Selection route and decision-makers\n- Conflicts that must be declared\n- Term, review and possible renewal\n\nThe opportunity should be communicated in a form appropriate to the people Kani hopes to reach. Access support should be available where relevant.\n\n## How is the decision made?\n\nThe appointment group:\n\n1. Reviews the role brief and agreed criteria.\n2. Declares personal, professional and financial conflicts.\n3. Considers applications, nominations or invited candidates.\n4. Seeks relevant perspectives without circulating private information unnecessarily.\n5. Records the decision and its reasons.\n6. Confirms compensation, authority, support and review arrangements.\n7. Communicates the outcome respectfully.\n\nA person does not participate in deciding their own appointment, pay, renewal or performance review.\n\nFor invited appointments, Kani records why the invitation is appropriate. For open processes, all candidates are assessed against the same stated criteria.\n\n## What terms apply?\n\n| Role | Proposed term |\n|---|---|\n| Council member | One year initially, renewable through review, with staggered handovers where possible |\n| Council Convenor | One year, with no additional authority beyond the Convenor’s stated responsibilities |\n| Advisory Circle member | One year, stating contribution, time, honorarium and review point |\n| Continuing Working Team role | Continuing appointment with an initial review period and regular role review |\n| Programme lead or curator | The period defined by the programme brief, including preparation, delivery and handover |\n| Temporary specialist | The period and deliverables defined in the agreement |\n\nRenewal is not automatic. It considers contribution, conduct, capacity, changing organisational needs and the value of bringing in new perspectives.\n\n## How are appointments reviewed?\n\nA review considers:\n\n- Whether the role remains necessary\n- Whether its mandate is still clear\n- Contribution and follow-through\n- Workload, support and compensation\n- Relationships and working conduct\n- Conflicts of interest\n- Learning and development\n- Whether the role should continue, change, conclude or be reopened\n\nThe person being reviewed receives the relevant feedback and an opportunity to respond.\n\n## How can an appointment conclude early?\n\nAn appointment may conclude because:\n\n- The agreed work has finished\n- The role is no longer required or funded\n- The person resigns\n- Responsibilities are repeatedly not fulfilled\n- A conflict makes the role untenable\n- Serious conduct or misuse-of-authority concerns are upheld\n- The appointment process or mandate is found to be invalid\n\nExcept where immediate protective action is necessary, the person receives notice of the concern, a fair opportunity to respond and a reasoned decision by non-conflicted people.\n\nInterim arrangements are time-limited and do not become permanent appointments without the appropriate process.\n\n## What is public and what remains private?\n\nKani may publish:\n\n- The names and mandates of Council members and advisors\n- Role titles and organisational responsibilities\n- Appointment routes and terms\n- Conflicts relevant to public decision-making\n\nApplications, references, personal information, compensation details and sensitive review material remain appropriately private.\n\nEvery concluding appointment includes a handover of responsibilities, records, relationships and organisational property.\n","structure-decisions":"Decision rules, financial limits and entity delegations must be collectively agreed before they become operating authority.\n\nDecisions should be made as close as possible to the people responsible for the work. Decentralisation depends on clear mandates, not the absence of accountability.\n\n## Who decides what?\n\n| Decision | Who decides |\n|---|---|\n| Routine work within an agreed role | The responsible person, consulting affected colleagues |\n| Develop or pilot a programme idea | The temporary programme review group convened for that proposal |\n| Proceed with a programme within agreed priorities and resources | The relevant non-conflicted reviewers, with the decision and conditions recorded |\n| Programme content within an approved brief | The appointed curator, creative lead or programme lead |\n| Delivery arrangements within approved resources | The producer or delivery lead |\n| Selection of programme participants or contributors | The people named in the approved programme brief |\n| Routine commercial booking within agreed pricing and capacity | The booking owner and Operations Coordinator |\n| Commissioned programme or programme design for another space | The relevant creative lead, Partnerships, Operations and Accounts, followed by required contractual authorisation |\n| Change affecting another team or shared resource | The affected leads together, coordinated by the Working Team |\n| Establish a new platform | The Council, informed by evidence from earlier programmes |\n| Annual priorities, overall allocations and major changes of direction | The Council |\n| Contracts, payments and other reserved commitments | The relevant authorised person or entity following the agreed decision |\n\nAdvisors, founders, partners and funders use these routes rather than giving parallel instructions.\n\nSee [Programme Selection](#programme-selection) for how programme proposals are reviewed.\n\n## What is a temporary programme review group?\n\nA review group is assembled for a particular proposal. It is not another standing committee.\n\nIt normally includes:\n\n- The Programme Coordinator\n- The relevant creative, research or programme lead\n- The Operations Coordinator\n- Community & Care when participation, access or representation is significant\n- Partnerships and Accounts when funding, pricing, contracts or reporting are involved\n- Relevant practitioners, knowledge holders or advisors where specialist judgement is needed\n\nThe group should be no larger than the proposal requires. The people developing an idea may explain and discuss it, but no proposal should be approved by its originator alone.\n\n## How is agreement reached?\n\nFor a collective decision:\n\n1. Explain the proposal, its purpose, resources and consequences.\n2. Hear the people affected and clarify questions.\n3. Identify conflicts of interest.\n4. Revise the proposal where concerns reveal a better approach.\n5. Check agreement explicitly.\n6. Record the decision, conditions, responsible people and review date.\n\nConsensus means reaching a proposal everyone involved can support or live with. It does not require everyone to prefer the same option.\n\nSilence is not agreement. A person may accept a proposal with reservations, which are recorded. Agreeing not to block a proposal does not commit someone to work they have not accepted.\n\nFor practical and reversible decisions, the group may agree to a time-limited trial.\n\n## When does a decision go to the Council?\n\nA matter goes to the Council when it:\n\n- Changes Kani’s purpose, direction or public commitments\n- Establishes or concludes a continuing platform\n- Requires a major or unallocated use of resources\n- Creates a long-term institutional obligation\n- Affects the organisation’s governance or legal responsibilities\n- Cannot be resolved within existing mandates\n- Presents a significant ethical, financial or reputational risk\n\nA proposal is not escalated merely because it is creatively ambitious or because one participant prefers a Council decision.\n\n## How does the Council decide?\n\nCouncil quorum is two-thirds of the appointed membership, rounded up.\n\nThis means:\n\n- Five members when the Council has seven\n- Six members when the Council has eight\n- Six members when the Council has nine\n\nThe quorum must include:\n\n- At least one Working Team representative\n- At least one community or youth representative\n- At least one founding or enabling-entity representative\n- At least one independent representative when independent seats have been appointed\n\nCouncil members receive proposals in advance and may raise concerns when unable to attend.\n\nCouncil decisions require explicit agreement among eligible participating members. The Convenor has no casting vote.\n\nIf agreement cannot be reached, the Council may:\n\n- Revise the proposal\n- Seek additional information or facilitation\n- Approve a smaller time-limited trial\n- Defer the matter\n- Decide not to proceed\n\nMembers with a conflict step aside. If too few eligible members remain to meet quorum, the decision waits for a properly constituted meeting.\n\n## How are decisions recorded?\n\nThe record states:\n\n- What was decided\n- Why the decision was made\n- Who participated\n- Any declared conflicts\n- Conditions or limits\n- Who is responsible for the next step\n- When the decision will be reviewed\n\nThe level of detail should match the importance of the decision. Sensitive personal information is not included in public records.\n\n## What happens in an urgent situation?\n\nProgramme and operational plans identify who may pause an activity or take immediate action to protect people, the premises or essential services.\n\nEmergency spending authority has a stated limit. The person acting records what happened and reports promptly.\n\nUrgency does not create a general right to bypass agreed responsibilities.\n","structure-operations":"Kani maintains shared systems and services so programmes can draw on dependable support without recreating the same infrastructure each time.\n\n## What shared functions are required?\n\n| Function | Who carries the work | Main responsibilities |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Programme pipeline** | Programme Coordinator | Proposal records, review status, approved briefs, review points and programme learning |\n| **Programme calendar** | Programme and Operations Coordinators | Dates, capacity, dependencies and connections between activities |\n| **Spaces and equipment** | Operations Coordinator with technical support | Bookings, readiness, maintenance, inventory, operators and changeovers |\n| **Institutional partnerships and funding** | Partnerships lead | Relationships, proposals, agreements and reporting, supported by relevant advisors |\n| **Commercial enquiries** | Named commercial or booking owner | Enquiries, qualification, quotations, visits and follow-up |\n| **Accounts** | Accounts team | Costing, financial records, invoices, payments and understandable budget reports |\n| **Design & communications** | Design & Communications | Visual identity, website, printed and digital material, confirmed programme information and documentation standards |\n| **Social media and audiences** | Social Media & Audience Coordinator | Content calendar, platform publishing, programme campaigns, audience enquiries, consent, asset coordination and engagement review |\n| **Hospitality** | Hospitality team | Accommodation, arrivals, food and guest support within agreed capacity |\n| **Community relationships and care** | Community & Care with programme leads | Participation, access, feedback, welcome and continuing relationships |\n| **Technical support** | Internal or appointed specialists | Sound, projection, digital systems, fabrication, installation and equipment operation |\n\nA function may initially be carried within another paid role, but every responsibility needs a named owner.\n\n## How are institutional partnerships managed?\n\nAdvisors, Council members, founders and programme leads may open relationships. The Partnerships lead owns organisational follow-through.\n\nFor each relationship:\n\n- A named person maintains contact\n- The purpose and possible programme are recorded\n- Programme leads shape the content\n- Operations checks capacity\n- Accounts confirms costs and reporting requirements\n- Community & Care contributes where participation or representation is involved\n- An authorised person makes the final contractual commitment\n\nAn introduction does not become a Kani commitment until it passes through this process.\n\n## How are commercial enquiries handled?\n\nCommercial enquiries may involve:\n\n- Space hire\n- Hospitality and stays\n- Retreats and offsites\n- Facilitated creative experiences\n- Commissioned publications or cultural projects\n- Programme design and curation for another space\n\nThe commercial or booking owner receives and qualifies the enquiry.\n\nThe relevant people then determine:\n\n- What the client is purchasing\n- Whether the work is consistent with Kani’s ethos\n- Whether Kani is advising, co-producing or delivering\n- Who holds creative and curatorial authority\n- What staff, space, equipment and relationships are required\n- Whether the price recovers the complete cost\n- Whether the work displaces existing commitments\n- What contract, payment and reporting arrangements are needed\n\nFor a programme designed for another space, a named creative or curatorial lead owns the content. Partnerships holds the client relationship, Operations checks feasibility, Accounts supports costing and an authorised person signs the agreement.\n\n## How are budgets managed?\n\nThe Council agrees annual priorities and overall allocations, informed by available funding and team capacity.\n\nEvery programme has:\n\n- An approved budget\n- A named budget holder\n- Clear spending authority and limits\n- A view of committed, available and spent funds\n- An agreed approach to changes and contingencies\n- Any required financial and narrative reporting\n\nRoutine expenditure proceeds within agreed limits. Transfers affecting another programme or shared resource require consultation and the appropriate approval.\n\nApproval, payment and financial recording should involve distinct people wherever possible.\n\nCommercial work accounts for its full demands on Kani, including staff time, utilities, setup, maintenance, hospitality and recovery time.\n\n## How are spaces and shared resources allocated?\n\nOne calendar and resource plan covers:\n\n- Public programmes\n- Residencies and learning activities\n- Festivals and platforms\n- Commercial bookings\n- Guest stays\n- Maintenance and technical work\n- Setup, changeover and recovery time\n\nA confirmed booking or programme includes its complete use of the place—not only the hours visible to participants.\n\nPublic-interest and community programmes receive protected time. Commercial work should support Kani’s continuity without gradually taking over its calendar.\n\n## What records and systems are maintained?\n\nKani maintains proportionate records for:\n\n- Programme proposals and decisions\n- Appointments and mandates\n- Budgets and financial commitments\n- Contracts and permissions\n- Spaces and equipment\n- Institutional and community relationships\n- Consent, access and care requirements\n- Documentation and programme learning\n- Concerns and follow-up where appropriate\n\nPersonal contact details and sensitive information are kept in suitable internal systems with appropriate access and consent.\n\nRecords should support continuity and accountability without turning people or relationships into administrative data alone.\n\n## How do enabling entities relate to operations?\n\nThe responsibilities of SoUF, KiLab and M/s Kani Home—including contracts, payments, employment, property and authorised signatures—must be documented alongside the Council and Working Team mandates.\n\nEveryone should be able to understand:\n\n- Which entity is undertaking an agreement\n- Who may sign or authorise it\n- Which budget and account apply\n- Who is operationally responsible\n- What reporting or legal obligations follow\n\nEntity authority should be transparent. It should not become an informal route for overriding agreed programme or governance decisions.\n","structure-accountability":"Named contacts and detailed review arrangements must be established before this becomes an operating process.\n\nCare is a shared responsibility across Kani. It includes respectful working, realistic workloads, fair compensation, accessible participation, attention to consent and a safe route for raising concerns.\n\nDisagreement is part of working together. Kani aims to address it early and fairly without making people fear the loss of work, access or belonging.\n\n## What kinds of concerns may arise?\n\n| Type of concern | Starting route |\n|---|---|\n| **Creative or practical disagreement** | The people directly responsible discuss it against the approved brief and available resources |\n| **Conflict over spaces, time, budgets or responsibilities** | Relevant Working Team members clarify commitments and help develop an agreement |\n| **Concern about a selection or appointment process** | A non-conflicted person reviews whether the stated process and criteria were followed |\n| **Unresolved disagreement affecting Kani’s direction or policy** | The Council considers it through a facilitated discussion |\n| **Concern about conduct, harassment, discrimination, retaliation or misuse of authority** | A confidential route involving an impartial contact |\n| **Concern involving a Council member, founder or usual contact** | An alternative contact outside the immediate relationship |\n\nDifferent concerns require different responses. Serious conduct concerns are not treated as ordinary creative disagreements.\n\n## How is a working disagreement addressed?\n\nFirst clarify what is disputed:\n\n- The goal or intended outcome\n- Decision authority\n- Resources or workload\n- Behaviour or communication\n- An unmet commitment\n- The interpretation of an agreed brief\n\nThe people involved explain what they need and consider workable options. A colleague may facilitate when everyone is comfortable with that arrangement.\n\nIf the matter remains unresolved, it goes to the appropriate Working Team responsibility or, where it exceeds their mandate, the Council.\n\nA person directly involved in the disagreement does not act as its impartial facilitator.\n\nAny agreement records what will happen, who is responsible and when it will be reviewed.\n\n## What if someone questions a selection or appointment?\n\nA person may raise a concern when they believe:\n\n- The published process was not followed\n- Criteria changed without explanation\n- A conflict of interest was not declared\n- Relevant information was ignored\n- Discrimination or retaliation affected the outcome\n- A person acted beyond their appointment authority\n\nThe review examines the fairness and integrity of the process. It does not automatically replace a curatorial or professional judgement merely because someone disagrees with the result.\n\nA review may:\n\n- Confirm that the process was followed\n- Require an explanation or correction\n- Reopen part or all of the process\n- Appoint new non-conflicted decision-makers\n- Recommend changes for future selections\n\nSensitive applicant and appointment information remains private.\n\n## How can someone raise a sensitive concern?\n\nKani names:\n\n- A confidential contact\n- An alternative contact outside the immediate working relationship\n- A route to independent or external support when required\n\nPeople may approach the alternative directly when a concern involves their usual contact, a programme lead, founder, Council member or someone with authority over their work.\n\nNo one is required to confront the person concerned before seeking support. Mediation is voluntary and is not assumed to be appropriate for serious misconduct.\n\nBefore information is shared, the person raising the concern is told:\n\n- Who may need access\n- Why the information is needed\n- What can and cannot remain confidential\n- What the next step may be\n\nSensitive concerns are not placed in public meeting minutes.\n\n## What should the response provide?\n\nThe process should provide:\n\n- Prompt acknowledgement\n- A clear next step and approximate timeframe\n- Practical support where needed\n- Protection against retaliation\n- An impartial assessment\n- A fair opportunity for relevant people to be heard\n- A reasoned outcome\n- An independent route to request review\n\nImmediate protective action may be taken when necessary for safety or to prevent retaliation. Such action is not itself a final finding.\n\n## How are conflicts of interest handled?\n\nAnyone connected to an applicant, supplier, partner, disputed decision or person involved in a concern declares the relationship.\n\nA conflicted person may provide factual information but does not control the decision or review.\n\nIf conflicts leave too few eligible people internally, Kani seeks an appropriate external reviewer.\n\n## How are people in positions of authority held accountable?\n\nConcerns about a Council member, advisor, Working Team member or Programme Team lead are reviewed by non-conflicted people with authority appropriate to the role.\n\nThe review considers:\n\n- The person’s written mandate\n- The seriousness and pattern of the concern\n- Their explanation and available evidence\n- Harm or risk to other people and Kani\n- Whether unclear roles, poor information or unrealistic workloads contributed\n- What corrective, protective or organisational action is required\n\nPossible outcomes include clarification, support, changed responsibilities, a formal warning, facilitated repair, review of the appointment or conclusion of the role.\n\nAny replacement follows [Appointments, Terms & Review](#structure-appointments).\n\n## Can people use an external route?\n\nYes. People may use appropriate legal, professional, safeguarding or complaint routes without first exhausting Kani’s internal process.\n\nKani should not prevent someone from seeking independent advice or support.\n","heart-wellness":"## Heart as our foundation\n\nWhatever we do, we do with heart and grace. Heart is the ground beneath all three pillars: care for ourselves, one another and the places we inhabit. It shapes how we listen, create, learn and work together.\n\nHeart also has a living programme: experiences that help people explore who they are, practise who they wish to become and grow towards their potential. Reflection, creativity, companionship and sustained practice connect inner development with how we live and contribute to the world.\n\n## Two connected expressions\n\n| Expression | What it makes possible |\n|---|---|\n| Everyday culture of care | Welcome, belonging, listening, team check-ins, rest, recognition and practical support across Kani. |\n| Programmes for growth and becoming | Guided journeys, reflective residencies, creative practice, mentorship, mindful gatherings and community learning throughout the year. |\n\nHeart remains a foundation shared by the three pillars. Its programmes connect with research, residencies and learning, and can lead to creative experiences and public exchange.\n\n## Nurupa · an initiative in development\n\nNurupa is a proposed journey of remembering, practising and expressing our potential through play, reflection and creative practice. Its working white paper describes an offering of the **Qalb Center at Kani Home**, as part of **The Slow Thing Festival**. These programme identities and arrangements remain in development.\n\nParticipants imagine an aspirational self—an avatar—and explore its qualities through daily practice, supported by mentors and fellow participants. A craft or passion project gives that exploration a tangible form. The proposed winter-to-spring journey brings together on-site and online participation, with a spring sharing of what participants have practised and made.\n\n| Programme element | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Reflection and self-enquiry | Make room to notice habits, aspirations and possibilities through journaling, quiet time and conversation. |\n| Aspirational avatar and play | Explore ways of being through an imagined identity, cooperative games and shared discovery. |\n| Creative practice | Develop a craft or passion project through regular, sustained attention. |\n| Mentorship and companionship | Offer encouragement, listening and reflection within a supportive community. |\n| Rhythm, rest and mindful attention | Create space to slow down and practise with care and at a personally appropriate pace. |\n| Sharing and continuation | Share chosen work and learning, then continue, adapt, mentor or rest. |\n\nNurupa is one expression of Heart. Other programmes can take different forms while sharing the intention of helping people grow with care, agency and connection. The white paper is a working proposal, not a confirmed calendar or a promise of particular outcomes.\n\n## Year-round possibilities\n\n- Reflective residencies and seasonal journeys combining personal enquiry with creative practice.\n- Regular circles for listening, journaling, storytelling and shared reflection.\n- Mindfulness, movement and wellbeing sessions with appropriate facilitators.\n- Mentored craft and passion projects, with optional opportunities to share work.\n- Community gatherings that nurture belonging, mutual support and continued practice.\n\n## How programmes are held\n\nThe Programme Coordinator connects Heart initiatives to the wider programme. Each initiative has a named lead, defined facilitator and mentor responsibilities, allocated time and a budget. The person holding Community & Care connects programme delivery with everyday care across Kani.\n\nParticipation respects different beliefs, backgrounds and personal boundaries. People choose what they share and may opt out of activities. Recording, documentation and public sharing require clear consent; personal reflections are not automatically publication material. Facilitator preparation, participant support, privacy and any practice-specific safeguards must be agreed before launch.\n\nNurupa's calendar, cohort format, mentor appointments, access and pricing, online arrangements and support protocols remain open for collective development. See [Open decisions](#open-decisions). Learning from each programme belongs in [Learnings & Feedback](#learnings-feedback), using participant-approved or anonymised summaries.\n","infrastructure":"Shared infrastructure is what every programme can draw on. It describes capabilities, not additional posts.\n\n| Capability | What is available |\n|---|---|\n| **Spaces** | Level 1 gallery with track lights and four movable walls; Level 2 screening and AV room; Level 3 office and meeting facilities; guest house for up to 15 people. |\n| **Technical infrastructure** | Sound, projection, lighting, connectivity, equipment operation and maintenance. |\n| **Production** | Installation, fabrication coordination, event logistics, procurement and specialist vendors. |\n| **Design & communications** | Graphics, publicity, registrations, audience information, signage and documentation coordination. |\n| **Community participation** | Relationship records, access planning, partner follow-up and feedback. |\n| **Knowledge systems** | Archives, contributor information, consent, permissions and programme learning. |\n| **Hospitality** | Accommodation, food, arrivals and guest support. |\n| **Finance & administration** | Budgets, agreements, invoicing, payments and reporting records. |\n| **Heart & care** | Welcome, belonging, listening, practical support and programmes for growth and becoming. |\n\nThe [Working Team](#structure-people) names the continuing owners. [Operations & Resources](#structure-operations) explains how programmes book staff, spaces, equipment and funds.\n\nFor Kanifest, one resource plan covers venues, equipment, operators, setup and changeovers. The Festival Producer coordinates delivery with the Operations Coordinator and a named Technical & Infrastructure Lead. Festival support and specialist crew are included in the edition budget.\n","platforms":"Platforms are named, continuing structures through which related programmes can develop over time. They can hold multiple projects, editions, gatherings, collaborations and outputs while drawing on Kani’s shared team, spaces and infrastructure.\n\n## Current platforms\n\n| Platform | What it holds | Named creative responsibility |\n|---|---|---|\n| [Kanifest](#kani-fest) | An interdisciplinary festival developed through distinct editions, curatorial propositions and community participation. | Festival Curator; Festival Producer holds delivery. |\n| [Kani Publications](#publications) | Editorial projects, research outputs, field notes, books and other forms of documented knowledge. | Editor / Publishing Lead. |\n| [Curated Marketplaces](#marketplaces) | Recurring encounters connecting artisans, makers, demonstrations, workshops and cultural programming. | Marketplace Curator. |\n| **Sunset Baithaks** | Intimate sunset gatherings around music, poetry, performance, conversation and collective listening. | A named curator or host for each series or edition. |\n| **Kani Canteen and Food Lab** | Food experimentation, shared meals, local ingredients, culinary knowledge and feeding the community. | A named food-programme lead, supported by hospitality and invited practitioners. |\n\nEvery platform has an agreed purpose, owner, resource model and review point. Individual programmes within it still require a clear brief, budget and delivery responsibility.\n\nFormats such as exhibitions, workshops, residencies, meals, performances and publications do not automatically become platforms. They are ways in which programmes can take form.\n\nSee [Programme Selection](#programme-selection) for how work is chosen, [People & Roles](#structure-people) for delivery relationships and [Developing new initiatives](#new-initiatives) for the pilot pathway.\n","kani-fest":"## Purpose\n\nAn interdisciplinary festival with a fresh curatorial proposition for each edition.\n\n## Festival working map\n\n**Drag to move, use + / − to zoom, and select a box for its explainer.** Roles can be combined where time, skills and funding allow.\n\n```kani-map\n[Festival team]\n- Kanifest | Agreed brief, budget and resource plan\n  - Festival Curator | Artistic direction · edition appointment\n    - Arts & New Media Curator | Proposed paid fellowship\n    - Sound & Music Curator | Proposed paid fellowship\n    - Craft & Material Practices Curator | Proposed paid fellowship\n    - Film & Moving Image Curator | Proposed paid fellowship\n  - Festival Producer | Delivery · fixed-term appointment\n    - Festival Schedule Coordinator | Timetable and changes\n    - Technical & Infrastructure Lead | Equipment, operators and crew\n    - Venue, artist and volunteer coordination | Supported edition delivery\n[Shared resources]\n- Programme continuity | Handover between editions\n- Partnerships & Funding | Relationships and reporting\n- Finance | Budgets and payments\n- Communications & Documentation | Audience information, consent and learning\n- Community & Care | Participation, access and team support\n- Hospitality | Stays, food and guest support\n- Shared Spaces & Equipment | Venues, equipment and availability\n[Collaborations]\n- Festival Curator <> Festival Producer | Collaboration and shared support\n- Shared resources <> Festival Curator | Collaboration and shared support\n- Shared resources <> Festival Producer | Collaboration and shared support\n```\n\nThe Festival Curator owns artistic direction; the Festival Producer owns delivery. Neither automatically becomes a permanent post or council seat. The Programme Coordinator carries continuity between editions, while the Operations Coordinator connects the edition to shared resources.\n\n```kani-details\n| Role / step | Responsibilities and purpose | Working relationship |\n|---|---|---|\n| Kanifest | Sets the edition brief, budget and resource plan through the relevant programme, working-team and council decisions. | Financial and contractual commitments follow the agreed entity authority. |\n| Festival Curator | Shapes the artistic proposition, integrates the tracks and makes selections within the agreed brief and budget. | Works alongside the Festival Producer and mentors the track curators. |\n| Arts & New Media Curator | Develops arts and new-media projects and supplies clear briefs, budgets and technical needs. | Proposed paid curatorial fellowship. |\n| Sound & Music Curator | Develops sound and music programming and coordinates performance requirements. | Proposed paid curatorial fellowship. |\n| Craft & Material Practices Curator | Develops workshops and material enquiry with practitioners and communities. | Proposed paid curatorial fellowship. |\n| Film & Moving Image Curator | Develops screenings and moving-image conversations, including permissions and formats. | Proposed paid curatorial fellowship. |\n| Festival Producer | Owns the delivery plan, production budget tracking, suppliers, crew and on-site coordination. | Fixed-term, edition-funded appointment working alongside the Curator. |\n| Festival Schedule Coordinator | Maintains the confirmed timetable, session briefs, contacts and change log. | Edition delivery role accountable to the Producer. |\n| Technical & Infrastructure Lead | Owns the equipment plan, operators, setup and changeovers. | Works with Operations and funded technical crew. |\n| Venue, artist and volunteer coordination | Covers space readiness, visitor flow, contributor requirements, volunteer induction, rotas and welfare. | Split into named roles according to the edition’s scale and budget. |\n| Programme continuity | Carries relationships, records and learning between editions. | Year-round Programme Coordinator with a documented festival handover. |\n| Partnerships & Funding | Develops appropriate partner and funder relationships and coordinates reporting. | Partnerships lead supported by relevant advisors. |\n| Finance | Tracks the edition budget, invoices, payments and reporting records. | Accounts team; approval follows delegated authority. |\n| Communications & Documentation | Publishes confirmed information and coordinates consent, credits, archives and learning. | Shared Design & Communications with edition support where funded. |\n| Community & Care | Supports local participation, access, welcome and practical team care. | Named continuing owner plus additional edition support where required. |\n| Hospitality | Coordinates accommodation, food, arrivals and guest support. | Existing Hospitality team with one festival contact. |\n| Shared Spaces & Equipment | Confirms venues, operators, setup, movement and changeovers. | Operations Coordinator with the Producer and technical lead. |\n```\n\n## Programming flow\n\n```kani-flow\n- Curators develop proposals | Content, contributors and requirements\n- Creative and resource review | Festival Curator + Festival Producer\n- Confirmed schedule | Festival Schedule Coordinator\n```\n\n```kani-details\n| Role / step | Responsibilities and purpose | Working relationship |\n|---|---|---|\n| Curators develop proposals | Each proposal states its purpose, participants, space, duration, budget, equipment and support needs. | The Festival Curator brings the tracks into one edition. |\n| Creative and resource review | The Curator and Producer test artistic coherence and deliverability before commitments are made. | Unresolved scope or resource questions use the route in [Decisions & Programme Authority](#structure-decisions). |\n| Confirmed schedule | One timetable records sessions, owners, setup and resource movements. | Changes are checked with affected leads and communicated once. |\n```\n\n## Volunteer pathway\n\n```kani-flow\n- Event volunteer | Defined tasks and supported shifts\n- Returning volunteer / team captain | Bounded responsibility and training\n- Paid associate / fellowship | Funded role with a clear brief\n```\n\n```kani-details\n| Role / step | Responsibilities and purpose | Working relationship |\n|---|---|---|\n| Event volunteer | Joins with induction, an agreed shift, breaks and a named lead. | An entry point; continuing participation is optional. |\n| Returning volunteer / team captain | Takes a bounded peer-support or coordination responsibility with training. | Sustained year-round work is scoped and paid. |\n| Paid associate / fellowship | Holds a defined brief, supervision, learning outcomes, time and compensation. | Prior volunteering is neither required nor a guarantee of selection. |\n```\n\n## Curatorial fellowships and working rhythm\n\nThe four tracks are a working framework. They may be combined, and poetry, theatre, talks and cross-track work must be placed explicitly. Proposed fellowships cover research, local relationships, programme development, delivery, reflection and handover; fees, time, mentoring and production support are agreed before recruitment.\n\nPreparation begins early enough for the fellowship cycle. Each edition budgets for production, outreach, documentation, evaluation and handover. See [People & Roles](#structure-people), [Operations & Resources](#structure-operations) and [Open decisions](#open-decisions).\n","publications":"## Purpose\n\nA proposed year-round publishing imprint serving work across all three pillars. “Kani Publications” is a working name.\n\n## Structure\n\n```kani-chart\n- Kani Publications | Agreed editorial plan and budget\n  - Editor / Publishing Lead | Editorial direction and quality\n    - Authors & contributors | Develop work with the editor\n    - Editorial specialists | Review, editing and translation as needed\n```\n\nThe Editor owns editorial quality within the agreed brief and budget. Programme coordination, Design & Communications, outreach, Partnerships, Accounts and distribution support are shared services rather than additional reporting lines.\n\n```kani-details\n| Role | Responsibility | Arrangement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Kani Publications | Sets priorities, resources and appointments through the annual programme and budget. | Programme responsibility within How Kani Works. |\n| Editor / Publishing Lead | Develops the editorial programme, manuscripts, review, sources, credit and permissions. | Retained or appointed per title or series. |\n| Authors & contributors | Develop original work and agree source, credit and permission arrangements. | Commissioned or agreed per publication. |\n| Editorial specialists | Provide subject review, language editing and translation. | Commissioned according to need. |\n```\n\n## Possible formats\n\n- Research papers, essays, field notes and residency journals.\n- Artists’ books, experimental editions, catalogues and festival readers.\n- Craft documentation, oral histories and community-authored work.\n- Learning resources, zines, audio publications and interactive formats.\n\n## Working arrangements\n\nStart with a small funded programme. Every title has a named editor, readership, format, budget, permissions and distribution plan. Programme leads contribute subject knowledge; shared services are allocated explicitly.\n\nBudget for editing, design, review, translation, production, outreach and distribution. Decide whether each title is free, sold, commissioned or partner-supported before production begins.\n","marketplaces":"## Purpose\n\nA recurring public programme bringing selected artisans and makers together with demonstrations, workshops and cultural activity.\n\nThe working ambition is a 3–4 day edition every two months, subject to demand, staffing and funding. Year 1 frequency remains open.\n\n## Structure\n\n```kani-chart\n- Curated Marketplaces | Agreed series brief and budget\n  - Marketplace Curator | Theme, selection and programme\n  - Delivery coordinator | Schedule, logistics and follow-up\n```\n\nThe curator owns selection. The delivery coordinator organises the edition and shared support; fees and booking administration do not determine curatorial acceptance.\n\n```kani-details\n| Role | Responsibility | Arrangement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Curated Marketplaces | Holds the series scope, calendar, budget and review. | Programme responsibility within How Kani Works. |\n| Marketplace Curator | Selects participants and shapes themes, demonstrations, workshops and cultural activity. | Series or edition appointment. |\n| Delivery coordinator | Coordinates schedules, requirements, logistics and follow-up. | Allocated working-team time or project-funded support. |\n```\n\n## Working arrangements\n\nConfirm frequency, selection criteria, stall arrangements, staffing, outreach and budget before announcing a series. Operations, communications, partnerships, accounts and hospitality are allocated through the shared resource plan.\n\nAfter each edition, review participant experience, access, attendance, costs and maker feedback. Record what changes and who maintains relationships between editions.\n","new-initiatives":"Use a simple progression:\n\n1. Identify an enquiry, need or opportunity connected to the ethos and pillars.\n\n2. Define a small pilot with a named owner, audience, budget and outcome. Build outreach into its development: listen to intended participants, identify access needs and agree who maintains the relationships afterwards.\n\n3. Deliver and document the pilot.\n\n4. Review value, local relevance, participation, workload and financial viability.\n\n5. Repeat or revise where justified.\n\n6. Establish a continuing platform only when there is a clear reason and capacity to sustain it.\n","learnings-feedback":"## A place to listen and learn\n\n**Status: Feedback collection is ongoing.** This section holds programme experience, questions and suggestions separately from the manifesto. The structure below is provisional and can develop as feedback comes in.\n\n## Kani Fest · Edition 1\n\nThe first edition ran for 15 days, with parallel workshops, talks and musical performances. Around 20 volunteers supported different tasks.\n\nThe notes below come from the organiser’s account shared during development of this document. They are initial observations, not a completed evaluation or a consensus from participants. Further feedback may add to or change them.\n\n| Area | Initial observation | Questions to explore |\n|---|---|---|\n| Programming | Programming and parallel workshops and talks worked well. | What felt valuable to participants and contributors? Where did sessions overlap productively or compete? |\n| Making and craft | Hands-on and craft workshops attracted strong interest. | Which formats, timings and facilitation worked best? What support or materials were missing? |\n| Sound and music | Local musicians drew the largest crowds. | What made these gatherings accessible and engaging? How did artists experience them? |\n| Timing | Around seven mornings were quiet across the 15-day edition. | Was this connected to timing, communication, audience availability or programme format? |\n| Infrastructure | Shared equipment moved between spaces, and infrastructure was understaffed. | Where did clashes or delays occur? What crew, equipment and changeover time would have helped? |\n| Production | There was no Festival Producer, and production capacity was weak. | Which responsibilities lacked an owner or adequate time? What needs dedicated coordination? |\n| Volunteers | Volunteers supported varied tasks; some are interested in greater responsibility across the year. | What felt supported or difficult? Who wants to stay involved, and in what capacity? |\n\n### Perspectives still to gather\n\nInvite feedback from participants, local communities, artists, workshop facilitators, volunteers, curators, production and technical crew, hospitality, partners and the organising team. Ask what worked, what was difficult and what they would change.\n\n## A simple feedback structure\n\nUse one record for each distinct observation or suggestion. Keep the original feedback separate from our interpretation and any decision.\n\n| Field | What to record |\n|---|---|\n| Programme / edition / date | Where and when the experience took place. |\n| Perspective and source | Participant, contributor, volunteer, team or partner; how the feedback was collected. |\n| Observation or feedback | What the person experienced, in their own words where appropriate. |\n| Theme | Programme, access, communication, timing, infrastructure, volunteer experience, hospitality, Heart & Wellness or another relevant theme. |\n| Question or suggestion | What needs understanding, testing or changing. |\n| Status | Collected, exploring, agreed for action or deferred. |\n| Response and follow-up | What was decided, why, who will follow up and when it will be reviewed. |\n\n### Working statuses\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Collected | Recorded; not yet reviewed with the relevant people. |\n| Exploring | More perspectives, detail or a small test are needed. |\n| Agreed for action | A response has been agreed, with an owner and review point. |\n| Deferred | Kept for later, with the reason recorded. |\n\n## Feedback record\n\nThe initial Edition 1 notes above are the starting record. Further participant and team feedback is still being collected; no individual submissions or agreed actions have been entered here yet.\n\nUse **Edit this chapter** to add reviewed notes. This page is a shared record, not a submission form. The collection method and final feedback format remain open for discussion. Keep personal concerns and identifiable sensitive feedback out of the public record; publish agreed or anonymised summaries where appropriate.\n\nAgreed changes to the manifesto should also be recorded in [Version history](#version-history), with the relevant pages updated together. Unresolved structural questions belong in [Open decisions](#open-decisions).\n\n\n## Heart programmes · learning to gather\n\nFor Nurupa and similar initiatives, invite participants to reflect on belonging, choice, mentor support, accessibility, creative practice and what they wish to carry forward. Use voluntary check-ins and end-of-programme reflections; do not rank personal growth or assume transformation. Record only consented or anonymised summaries here. No Nurupa participant feedback has been collected in this document yet.\n","structure-archive":"Earlier council, governance and staffing proposals are preserved here as a record of how the thinking developed. They are **archived references**, not additional bodies or staffing commitments to combine with the current proposal.\n\nFor the latest proposed model, start with [How Kani Works — Overview](#structure).\n\n## Structure pages consolidated after v0.11\n\nThe separate Governance, Advisory Circle, Working Team, Programme Teams and Disagreements pages from v0.11 were consolidated into the current six-page section.\n\n| Page | What it preserves |\n|---|---|\n| [Governance — v0.11](#structure-governance) | The earlier separate Council and governance page. |\n| [Advisory Circle — v0.11](#structure-advisory) | The earlier standalone advisory model. |\n| [Working Team — v0.11](#structure-working-team) | The earlier continuing-team description. |\n| [Programme Teams — v0.11](#structure-programme-teams) | The earlier programme-delivery page. |\n| [Disagreements & Resolution — v0.11](#structure-resolution) | The earlier disagreements and complaints process. |\n\n## Earlier council and governance proposals\n\n| Page | What it preserves |\n|---|---|\n| [Council · Option 1](#council) | The earlier advisory-area model. |\n| [Council · Option 2](#council-option-2) | A participatory council with serving curators as ex-officio members. |\n| [Council · Option 3](#council-option-3) | The 16-member shared-stewardship council, six advisors and Working Coordination Group. |\n| [Earlier decision rights](#decisions) | Decision responsibilities associated with the earlier models. |\n| [Enabling entities — earlier reference](#entities) | The earlier description of institutional relationships and responsibilities. |\n\n## Earlier staffing proposals\n\n| Page | What it preserves |\n|---|---|\n| [Year 1 · Minimum team](#year-one) | The Director-based starting team and specialist support. |\n| [Full structure](#full-structure) | The earlier growth model with dedicated functional roles. |\n| [How the team grows](#growth) | The earlier approach to phasing appointments. |\n\nThe original chapter links still work. Their text is preserved, with an archive notice to distinguish them from [How Kani Works](#structure).\n\n## Preserved editions\n\n[Read the complete v0.8 edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.8.html), before the structure section and this archive were introduced.\n\n[Version history](#version-history) records earlier editions and the reasons for each change. Published snapshots remain unchanged.\n","open-decisions":"This edition incorporates the agreed drafting changes. The following questions still need collective agreement before they become operating commitments.\n\n- [ ] Review the ethos wording, three pillar titles and activity menus with the group.\n- [ ] Review [How Kani Works](#structure): the seven-to-nine-member Council, representative selection, continuing roles and the six-month review.\n- [ ] Confirm the six Advisory Circle areas in [People & Roles](#structure-people), initial appointments, time commitments, honoraria and the route for responding to advice.\n- [ ] Adopt [Appointments, Terms & Review](#structure-appointments), including appointment routes, review periods and handovers.\n- [ ] Agree decision rules, quorum, conflicts and recusals, emergency authority, financial limits and entity delegations before using them.\n- [ ] Establish confidential contacts, an alternative route and impartial complaint and review arrangements.\n- [ ] Once the structure is adopted, align detailed programme charts and earlier reporting terminology across the manifesto.\n- [ ] Confirm the proposed platforms—Kanifest, Kani Publications, Curated Marketplaces, Sunset Baithaks, and Kani Canteen and Food Lab—and the publishing imprint.\n- [ ] Review and adopt the Programme Selection process, including who records decisions and how community participation is included.\n- [ ] Choose a realistic Year 1 calendar, including festival scope, marketplace frequency and publication commitments and editorial focus.\n- [ ] Confirm outreach priorities, initial community and institutional relationships, allocated staff time and participation-support budgets.\n- [ ] Confirm Year 1 staffing and support budgets under [How Kani Works](#structure); agree platform staffing against actual programmes.\n- [ ] Agree Kani Fest duration and timetable; confirm the Producer, technical lead, shared-resource plan and delivery budget.\n- [ ] Agree curatorial fellowship fees, time commitments, mentoring, selection process and programme support before recruitment.\n- [ ] Confirm volunteer support, team-captain scopes and any funded year-round associate opportunities.\n- [ ] Name the Heart & Wellness coordinator, allocate time and agree support and alternative routes for concerns.\n- [ ] Develop Nurupa within Heart: confirm the Qalb Center / Slow Thing Festival relationship, programme lead, mentor preparation, calendar, cohort format, budget, pricing and local access, privacy and recording consent, online participation and participant-support protocols before launch.\n- [ ] Confirm entity responsibilities and delegated financial authority.\n- [ ] Confirm how publishing sales and distribution will be administered initially.\n\nCollect programme and presentation feedback in [Learnings & Feedback](#learnings-feedback). A suggestion is not an agreed operating decision.\n\n","version-history":"## Current edition\n\n**v0.13 · Revised draft — For discussion**\n\n**Published:** 12 November 2026\n\nAdded Social Media & Audience Coordination as a continuing, paid Working Team function.\n\nSeparated audience-facing digital communication from visual design and from long-term community engagement, while keeping the three functions connected.\n\n## Editions and changes\n\n| Edition | What changed | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| v0.13 · Revised draft · 12 November 2026 | Added Social Media & Audience Coordination to the Working Team and clarified its relationship with Design & Communications, programme teams and Community & Care. | Give Kani’s year-round digital presence, programme campaigns, audience enquiries and engagement review clear paid ownership without confusing social media with community engagement. |\n| v0.12 · Revised draft · 12 November 2026 | Rewrote the three pillar pages and outreach; expanded Programme Selection; added external programme-design services; consolidated the structure into How Kani Works; and introduced Appointments, Terms & Review. | Make the programme pathways, people selection, governance and operating responsibilities clearer without adding another management layer. |\n| v0.11 · Revised draft · 12 November 2026 | Clarified the opening and ethos; added a linked structure synopsis; reframed pillars, programmes and platforms; added two platforms and a Programme Selection process. | Make Kani’s values, organisation and programme-development decisions easier to understand. |\n| v0.10 · Revised draft · 11 November 2026 | Restored the Advisory Circle and consolidated repeated team, outreach, infrastructure and platform content. | Keep specialist expertise visible while making the manifesto shorter and internally consistent. |\n| v0.9 · Revised draft · 11 November 2026 | Added The Structure and its seven subpages; archived earlier governance and staffing proposals with original links preserved. | Make the current proposal easier to understand without rebuilding the wider menu. |\n| v0.8 · Revised draft · 11 November 2026 | Added shared-stewardship Option 3, lean coordination roles and detailed governance procedures; linked related chapters. | Explore decentralised authority without creating four director posts. |\n| v0.7 · Revised draft · 11 November 2026 | Expanded Heart into a programme space; introduced Nurupa and linked relevant chapters. | Make personal growth and becoming part of the lived foundation, alongside everyday care. |\n| v0.6 · Revised draft · 4 November 2026 | Added a participatory council option and side-by-side comparison; retained Option 1 and its reference decision rights; identified decisions required before adoption. | Explore a more inclusive model without prematurely replacing the earlier proposal. |\n| v0.5 · Revised draft · 2 November 2026 | Three high-level dotted connections without numbered relationship controls; programming and volunteer flows with clickable detail instead of repeated visible tables. | Make the map and pathways easier to read while retaining the detailed responsibilities. |\n| v0.4 · Revised draft · 2 November 2026 | Draggable festival map with shared resources and numbered dotted collaboration links; separate programming and volunteer flows; Heart & Wellness above shared resources with content to be developed; Learnings & Feedback immediately before Review & Editing. | Make working relationships easier to explore and keep developing content easy to find. |\n| v0.3 · Revised draft · 2 November 2026 | Unified expandable festival map with shared resources; year-round Heart & Wellness activities; detailed roles retained; new Learnings & Feedback chapter with collection marked ongoing. Internal analogy and retrospective material removed from manifesto chapters. | Present the proposed organisation clearly while giving experience and evolving feedback their own place. |\n| v0.2 · Revised draft · 2 November 2026 | Clickable Kani Fest working map and role explainers; paid curatorial fellowship model; named delivery and infrastructure responsibilities; programming workflow; supported volunteer pathway; shared Heart & Wellness responsibility; aligned staffing, outreach and decision pages. | Build on first-edition experience and make responsibility, shared-resource planning and routes for participation clear. |\n| v0.1 · Alpha · 2 November 2026 | “An evolving manifesto”; clearer transdisciplinary approach; outreach section and responsibilities across the structure; one-year council appointments with one or two members per area; Sound & Music title; charts and responsibilities for all three platforms; simpler status and review notes. | Prepare a coherent presentation edition and make relationships, responsibilities and the evolution of thinking easier to follow. |\n| Initial website · 1 November 2026 | The first online working document, before numbered editions. | Establish an editable, shared source. |\n\n[Read the preserved v0.13 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.13.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.12 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.12.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.11 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.11.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.10 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.10.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.9 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.9.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.8 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.8.html).\n\n[Read the final v0.7 edition before Option 3](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.7-final.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.7 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.7.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.6 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.6.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.5 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.5.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.4 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.4.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.3 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.3.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.2 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.2.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.1 Alpha edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.1.html).\n\n[Read the initial website snapshot](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-initial.html). Its content is also preserved in the [original repository commit](https://github.com/bhaumikdebanshu/KaniManifesto/tree/14fac9b564864435f37587fa36fc6e175b9512d5).\n\nSnapshots stay as they were published. The main website carries the latest edition.\n\n## Simple versioning\n\n| Version | When to use it |\n|---|---|\n| v0.1 · Alpha | This first presentation edition. |\n| v0.2 · Revised draft | Earlier revision of the Kani Fest model. |\n| v0.3 · Revised draft | Earlier revision: unified map and separate learning and feedback. |\n| v0.4 · Revised draft | Earlier revision: draggable collaboration map and revised navigation. |\n| v0.5 · Revised draft | Earlier revision: simpler collaboration links and less repetition. |\n| v0.6 · Revised draft | Earlier revision: participatory council option and Heart foundation. |\n| v0.7 · Revised draft | Earlier revision: Heart programmes and Nurupa in development. |\n| v0.8 · Revised draft | Earlier revision: shared stewardship and a light Working Coordination Group. |\n| v0.9 · Revised draft | Earlier revision: The Structure and an archive of earlier proposals. |\n| v0.10 · Revised draft | Earlier revision: restored Advisory Circle and a crisper, aligned manifesto. |\n| v0.11 · Revised draft | Earlier revision: a clearer ethos, linked structure synopsis, programme framework and selection process. |\n| v0.12 · Revised draft | Current revision: clearer programme pathways, selection, appointments and a consolidated How Kani Works section. |\n| v1.0 · Agreed baseline | When the group agrees a structure to begin working with. It can still evolve. |\n\nDo not change the version for every typo. Increment it when the thinking, responsibilities or structure meaningfully changes. Record what changed, why and what remains unresolved; preserve the previous edition before publishing the next.\n\n## Learning and feedback\n\nProgramme observations and feedback now have a dedicated [Learnings & Feedback](#learnings-feedback) chapter. Collection is ongoing, and its structure remains provisional. Version history records changes made to the manifesto; feedback can remain under discussion without becoming an agreed change.\n\nKeep unresolved operating questions in [Open Decisions](#open-decisions). Check related chapters before publishing an agreed revision; see the [Document review checklist](#document-plan).\n\n\n","document-plan":"## Review one complete edition\n\nReview the manifesto, programme framework, advisory arrangements, team and platforms together. This prevents a change to one role from leaving contradictory responsibilities elsewhere.\n\n## Consistency checklist\n\n| When this changes | Also review |\n|---|---|\n| Ethos or pillars | Home, Programme Framework, pillar chapters, platform descriptions and open decisions. |\n| Programme selection criteria or process | Programme Framework, Programme Selection, People & Roles, Decisions & Programme Authority and open decisions. |\n| Council membership or authority | People & Roles, Appointments, Decisions & Programme Authority, enabling-entity arrangements and open decisions. |\n| Advisory areas or appointments | People & Roles, Appointments, Operations & Resources and open decisions. |\n| Outreach responsibilities | Outreach, People & Roles, Operations & Resources and relevant programme pages. |\n| Heart & Wellness | Home, Heart & Wellness, People & Roles, outreach and Care, Concerns & Accountability. |\n| Platform staffing or reporting | People & Roles, the relevant platform map or chart and Operations & Resources. |\n| Programme observations or feedback | Learnings & Feedback, unresolved decisions, and any agreed changes to the relevant manifesto chapters. |\n| A substantive agreed revision | Version label, version history, previous-edition snapshot, navigation, Markdown export and print view. |\n\nCharts distinguish programme responsibility from shared support. Platform leads and advisors are not additional permanent hires.\n\n## Review and publish\n\nCollect proposed edits, review them as a batch, then publish one coherent edition. Keep feedback in [Learnings & Feedback](#learnings-feedback), distinguishing collected observations from agreed actions. Preserve the presentation edition before incorporating feedback so readers can follow how the thinking changed.\n\nSee [Version history](#version-history) for the current edition and revision approach.\n","editing":"## Edit a chapter\n\n1. Open the chapter you want to change.\n2. Click **Edit this chapter** in the top bar. It opens the corresponding Markdown file on GitHub.\n3. Sign in to GitHub and make your changes. Headings use `##`; tables use rows separated by vertical bars.\n4. Use GitHub’s Preview tab to check the text and table formatting.\n5. Select **Commit changes**, add a short description, and save to the publishing branch if you have permission.\n6. Wait for the Pages deployment to complete, then refresh this website.\n\nThe editor link appears once the site is hosted on GitHub Pages, or when the repository is set in `site.json`.\n\n## Let someone else contribute\n\nShare the website link for reading. Give selected collaborators repository write access if they should edit directly. Others can propose changes through GitHub pull requests. This is repository editing, not simultaneous Google Docs-style editing.\n\n## Tables and checklists\n\nThe site displays tables and checklists from the Markdown files. Make changes in the source file through **Edit this chapter**. Checkboxes on the reading page are intentionally read-only; use `- [x]` in the source to mark a decision complete.\n\n## Download or print\n\n**Download Markdown** combines the current chapters into a single editable document. **Print all** prepares every chapter for printing or saving to PDF.\n\n## One working source\n\nAfter publishing, the Markdown files in this GitHub repository are the website’s working source. The earlier ChatGPT Page and Obsidian folder do not automatically synchronise with it.\n\n## Visibility\n\nThe free public-repository setup makes the site and its source public. The site requests that search engines do not index it, but this is not access control. Anyone with the address can read it.\n\n## Versions and charts\n\nUse [Version history](#version-history) for substantive changes. The main website is the current working edition; the linked snapshots preserve earlier editions and should not be edited. Use **Print all** on a snapshot to save that edition as a PDF.\n\nPlatform charts are written as indented bullet lists inside a `kani-chart` code block in each platform’s Markdown file. Indentation defines the project hierarchy. Kani Fest uses one `kani-map` block with three labelled sections: `[Festival team]`, `[Shared resources]` and `[Collaborations]`. Indented bullets define the team hierarchy; flat bullets define shared resources. Collaboration entries use `- Role A <> Role B | Relationship label`, matching box names exactly. The programming and volunteer flows use separate `kani-flow` blocks below the map. Each box title matches the first cell of its explanation table exactly; the next cells supply the explainer text. The programming and volunteer explanations are in `kani-details` blocks directly beneath their flows. These tables supply the clickable explainers and print output without repeating on the reading page. Edit those table cells to change an explainer. Keep map collaboration links at the level of the two festival leads and shared resources. Update the chart and table together when a role or relationship changes. Shared support stays separate from reporting lines.\n\nBefore publishing a substantive new edition, update the version and date in `site.json`, the history page and the preserved snapshot together. Follow the [Document review checklist](#document-plan).\n\nProgramme observations and feedback belong in [Learnings & Feedback](#learnings-feedback). Its collection format is provisional; use reviewed summaries when updating this public record.\n","structure-governance":"**Current proposal—for discussion.**\n\nThe Kani Council holds responsibility for the organisation’s shared direction and accountability. It connects people doing the work with community perspectives and external experience.\n\n## Council membership\n\n| Members | Seats | Contribution |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Working-team representatives | 2 | Experience of programmes, operations, capacity and everyday working conditions. |\n| Community representatives | 2 | Local knowledge, priorities, participation and access. |\n| Youth representative | 1 | Young people’s perspectives on learning, participation and Kani’s future. |\n| Founding and enabling-entity stewardship | 2 | Institutional continuity, legal and financial responsibilities, and transparent stewardship of the place. |\n| **Total** | **7** | Members share responsibility for Kani as a whole. |\n\nThese are council responsibilities, not seven additional staff posts. Each person occupies one seat.\n\nThe [Advisory Circle](#structure-advisory) is separate from the council. Advisors strengthen decisions but do not acquire governance votes or operational authority through their advisory appointment.\n\n## What the council does\n\nThe council:\n\n- Protects Kani’s purpose and ethos.\n- Agrees annual priorities and overall budget allocations.\n- Reviews programme outcomes, financial information and working conditions.\n- Agrees major partnerships and commitments within the relevant entity arrangements.\n- Establishes clear responsibilities and decision-making boundaries.\n- Oversees a fair process for appointing and reviewing continuing coordination roles.\n\nThe council does not select every artist, approve every purchase or issue competing instructions to staff.\n\n## Selection and terms\n\nWorking-team representatives are chosen by the working team. Community representatives are selected through an accessible process developed with local participants. Young people are involved in choosing the youth representative. Founding and enabling-entity representatives are named through a transparent process that states the authority they carry.\n\nFor the first council, the organising team facilitates a founding meeting involving staff and community participants, records how members were selected and publishes the proposed membership.\n\nMembers serve an initial one-year term, renewable through review. Staggered handovers help maintain continuity. Participation time, honoraria and access support are agreed in advance.\n\nRepresentatives gather views from the people they represent and report back on decisions.\n\n## Convening\n\nOne member serves as **Council Convenor** for a year. The Convenor gathers agenda items, ensures information is circulated and follows up on agreed actions.\n\nMeeting facilitation can rotate. The Convenor has no casting vote or unilateral power to override decisions. Every member can put a matter on the agenda.\n\nThe council meets quarterly, with additional meetings for decisions that cannot wait. Advisors join only the items where their contribution is useful. Members receive a short programme, finance and capacity update between meetings.\n\n## Founders and enabling entities\n\nFounders participate through defined roles. Any authority retained through ownership, funding or an enabling entity is stated openly.\n\nThe responsibilities of SoUF, KiLab and M/s Kani Home—including contracts, payments and authorised signatures—must be documented alongside the council’s mandate. Teams should know which decisions they can make and which need additional authorisation.\n\nSee the [Advisory Circle](#structure-advisory) for specialist contribution, [Decision-making](#structure-decisions) for agreement and quorum, and [Disagreements & Resolution](#structure-resolution) for conflicts and accountability.\n","structure-advisory":"**Current proposal—for discussion.**\n\nThe Advisory Circle brings experience, critical reflection and relationships into Kani without becoming another authority layer. The council governs, the working team runs Kani, programme teams deliver the work, and advisors strengthen all three.\n\n## Six areas of contribution\n\n| Area | Contribution |\n|---|---|\n| **Arts, Culture & Publishing** | Creative practice, curatorial mentoring, publishing and cultural relationships. |\n| **Research & Learning** | Residencies, fellowships, learning methods and research collaborations. |\n| **Systems & Organisational Development** | Governance, team structures, documentation and organisational learning. |\n| **Technology & Digital Practice** | Creative technology, digital infrastructure, archives and technical capability. |\n| **Business & Enterprise** | Revenue models, pricing, commercial experiences, hospitality and sustainability. |\n| **Institutional Partnerships & Fundraising** | Institutional networks, grants, CSR, sponsorship and resource mobilisation. |\n\nThese are six areas of contribution, not necessarily six separate people. One advisor may cover connected areas where the fit is strong. Kani can invite additional specialists for a particular question without expanding the standing circle.\n\n## What advisors do\n\nAdvisors:\n\n- Mentor relevant members of the working and programme teams.\n- Review major proposals and annual priorities when invited.\n- Build practitioner, institutional, funding and industry relationships.\n- Help strengthen systems and organisational capability.\n- Join temporary working groups where their expertise is useful.\n- Contribute an independent view to the annual review.\n\nAdvisors do not manage staff, approve routine spending, direct programme teams or override the council. An introduction or recommendation becomes work only when a named team member accepts responsibility for following it through.\n\n## Appointments and accountability\n\nEach advisor has a one-year appointment stating their area, expected contribution, time commitment, review point and any honorarium. Appointments are reviewed annually and can be renewed.\n\nThe circle meets together twice a year. Individual advisors work with relevant people between meetings. They may join council discussions but do not hold a council vote simply by being advisors. If an advisor also holds a council seat or paid role, the two responsibilities and any conflict of interest are recorded separately.\n\nThe council records its response to significant advice: accepted, being explored or not proceeding, with a brief reason. This gives advice a clear route into the organisation without turning it into an instruction.\n","structure-working-team":"**Current proposal—for discussion.** The following is a proposed structure, not a list of confirmed appointments.\n\nThe working team maintains continuity across Kani’s programmes and keeps the place functioning throughout the year.\n\n## Starting team\n\n| Role | Main responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| **Programme Coordinator** | Develops the year-round programme, connects initiatives, supports curators and facilitators, maintains programme relationships and carries learning between editions. |\n| **Operations Coordinator** | Maintains the shared calendar, resource plan, venue readiness, working processes and coordination with accounts, hospitality and technical support. |\n| **Design & Communications** | Maintains visual communication, programme announcements, audience information, registration communication and documentation coordination. |\n\nThese three roles work alongside the existing Accounts and Hospitality teams and draw on the [Advisory Circle](#structure-advisory) where specialist guidance or relationships are useful.\n\nTwo further responsibilities require explicit capacity:\n\n| Responsibility | Starting arrangement |\n|---|---|\n| **Partnerships & Fundraising** | A named part-time or retained person develops institutional relationships, funding opportunities, proposals and reporting. |\n| **Community & Care** | Allocated paid time within a suitable role, or part-time support, for local relationships, participation, feedback and practical team care. |\n\nCombining responsibilities is possible where skills and time allow. It must not become an assumption that additional work happens without support or compensation.\n\n## How the team works together\n\nThere is no separate Working Coordination Group. Coordination is part of the working team’s normal work.\n\nA fortnightly meeting reviews:\n\n- Upcoming programmes and commitments.\n- Staff capacity and support needs.\n- Space, equipment and scheduling conflicts.\n- Partnership and funding developments.\n- Decisions requiring council involvement.\n\nMeetings become more frequent during busy periods. An agreed person maintains the action list; this does not make them the head of the team.\n\nEach role has a short written mandate stating its responsibilities, decision authority, resources and review arrangements. Each person also has one named support and review contact, so feedback and instructions do not come from several directions.\n\nContinuing coordination-role reviews are overseen by the council with team input. Members do not participate in decisions about their own appointment, pay or performance.\n\n## How the team grows\n\nAdditional appointments follow demonstrated need and funding. They may include technical support, community engagement, commercial bookings or dedicated programme management.\n\nBefore adding a permanent role, consider whether the work is continuing, seasonal or specific to one programme. Festival peaks should carry their own staffing budget.\n\nSee [Programme Teams](#structure-programme-teams) for edition appointments and [Operations & Resources](#structure-operations) for shared support.\n","structure-programme-teams":"**Current proposal—for discussion.**\n\nProgramme teams bring particular initiatives to life. They draw on Kani’s shared resources while holding responsibility for their own creative and delivery work.\n\nEach programme begins with a short agreement covering:\n\n- Purpose, participants and intended contribution.\n- Named creative and delivery responsibilities.\n- Budget, funding and spending authority.\n- Staff time, spaces, equipment and technical support.\n- Timeline, communication, care and access needs.\n- Review, documentation and handover.\n\nFor a small initiative, one person may hold both creative and delivery responsibility. Larger programmes need distinct roles.\n\n## Kanifest\n\nKanifest follows the same model at a larger scale. The Festival Curator owns artistic direction; the Festival Producer owns delivery. Track curators and the edition team work within an agreed brief, budget and resource plan. The detailed team, programming flow and volunteer pathway are held once on the [Kani Fest page](#kani-fest).\n\nThe year-round Programme Coordinator holds continuity between editions. The Operations Coordinator connects the festival to shared spaces, equipment and services.\n\n## Connection to the council\n\nProgramme leads join council discussions concerning their brief, allocation, major changes and review. They can submit proposals and concerns directly and can draw on relevant advisors.\n\nProgramme appointments do not automatically create council seats. Invited leads contribute to the discussion; formal council decisions remain with its appointed members. Within an approved programme, authority remains with the programme team.\n\n## Other initiatives\n\nThe same approach applies to residencies, publications, marketplaces and Heart programmes. Each has a named lead, clear resources and an appropriate level of support.\n\nAt the end of a programme, the team hands over relationships, records, financial information and learning to the relevant continuing team member.\n\nSee the programme pages for their specific creative and delivery arrangements: [Kani Fest](#kani-fest), [Kani Publications](#publications), [Curated Marketplaces](#marketplaces) and [Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness).\n","structure-resolution":"**Current proposal—for discussion.** Named contacts and the detailed complaints and review arrangements must be established before this becomes an operating process.\n\nDisagreement is part of working together. Kani aims to address it early, fairly and without making people fear the loss of work, access or belonging.\n\nDifferent concerns need different responses.\n\n| Type of concern | Starting route |\n|---|---|\n| Creative or practical disagreement | The people directly responsible discuss it against the agreed brief and available resources. |\n| Conflict over spaces, time, budgets or responsibilities | The relevant working-team members help clarify commitments and develop an agreement. |\n| Unresolved disagreement affecting Kani’s direction or policy | The council considers it through a facilitated discussion. |\n| Concern about conduct, harassment, discrimination, retaliation or misuse of authority | A confidential route involving an impartial contact; it is not treated as an ordinary programme disagreement. |\n\n## Resolving a working disagreement\n\nFirst, clarify what is disputed: the goal, decision authority, resources, behaviour or an unmet commitment.\n\nThe people involved identify what they need and explore workable options. A colleague can facilitate if everyone is comfortable with that arrangement.\n\nIf the matter remains unresolved, it goes to the appropriate working-team lead or council. A person directly involved in the dispute does not act as its impartial facilitator.\n\nRecord any agreement, who will act and when it will be reviewed.\n\n## Raising a sensitive concern\n\nKani names a confidential contact and an alternative contact outside the immediate working relationship. People can approach the alternative directly when the concern involves their usual contact, a founder, a council member or someone with authority over their work.\n\nNo one is required to confront the person concerned before seeking support. Mediation is voluntary and is not assumed to be suitable for serious misconduct.\n\nBefore information is shared, explain who needs access and why. Sensitive complaints do not go into public meeting minutes.\n\nThe process should provide:\n\n- Prompt acknowledgement and a clear next step.\n- Practical support and protection against retaliation.\n- An impartial assessment, with external assistance where needed.\n- A fair opportunity for the people concerned to be heard.\n- A reasoned outcome and an independent route to request review.\n\nPeople can use appropriate external support or complaint routes without first exhausting Kani’s internal process.\n\n## Conflicts of interest and accountability\n\nAnyone connected to a supplier, applicant, partner or disputed decision declares that interest and steps aside where necessary.\n\nConcerns about a council member’s conduct or repeated failure to fulfil their role follow a documented review involving non-conflicted people. The member has an opportunity to respond. Any replacement follows the selection process for that seat.\n\nEach review should also ask whether unclear responsibilities, unrealistic workloads or poor access to information contributed to the problem, and what Kani needs to change.\n","entities":"The entities enable the work; the pillars describe activities. These are proposed operating remits, to be aligned with the entities’ actual mandates and approvals.\n\n| Entity | Proposed contribution |\n|---|---|\n| Seed of Unity Foundation — SoUF | Community, education, supported research, fellowships and public-interest programmes. |\n| Kashmir Innovation Labs — KiLab | Craft, material research, documentation, innovation and related collaborations. |\n| M/s Kani Home | Commercial experiences, space hire, hospitality and accommodation. |\n\nEvery project identifies its lead entity, budget and authorised signatory. Operational coordination under the Director does not replace the financial and contractual authority of the relevant entity.\n","council":"## Purpose\n\nThe council helps establish annual direction and priorities, contributes expertise, mentors practices and opens relationships. Senior advisors support the working team through key conversations and defined commitments. Day-to-day execution rests with staff and commissioned collaborators.\n\n## Seven advisory areas\n\nThe areas describe expertise, not named individuals or separate departments. Each area may have one or two members, depending on the expertise and support needed. Where two members share an area, they agree how to divide responsibilities and coordinate their contribution. Seven advisory areas can therefore have between seven and fourteen members when all areas are filled.\n\n| Advisory role | Main contribution |\n|---|---|\n| Art & Cultural Practice | Visual arts, film, exhibitions, artistic development and cultural relationships. |\n| Design, Systems & Technology | Programme formats, organisational systems, experiences, shared tools and infrastructure, and emerging technologies. |\n| Research, Learning & Publishing | Research direction, academic relationships, educational programmes and editorial standards. |\n| Sound & Music | Music programming, sound practices, practitioner networks and interdisciplinary collaborations. |\n| Craft, Heritage & Community | Artisan relationships, local knowledge, heritage, craft innovation and community participation. |\n| Business & Enterprise | Commercial models, pricing, retreats, bookings and market relationships. |\n| Fundraising & Resource Development | Grants, CSR relationships, sponsorships, philanthropy and institutional funding. |\n\nInstitutional partnerships are a shared responsibility across the council. Membership should include at least two people deeply connected to Kashmir’s communities and practices. Invite additional specialists for particular enquiries rather than expanding the standing council for every discipline.\n\n## Appointments and accountability\n\n- Council members serve one-year terms. Membership is reviewed annually, and appointments may be renewed.\n\n- The annual review considers contribution, agreed commitments, continuity and the expertise needed for the next year.\n\n- One member serves as Convenor for one year, coordinating agendas and follow-up without an additional artistic veto.\n\n- Each member has a written remit, agreed time commitment and compensation arrangement.\n\n- Each member undertakes a small set of concrete commitments, such as introducing partners, mentoring a project or helping develop a programme.\n\n- Departing members document commitments and hand over relationships to the working team.\n\n## Working method and disagreements\n\nHold quarterly planning and review meetings, with shorter project conversations as needed. The Director attends; relevant staff and platform leads join for their items. The Programme & Operations Coordinator initially provides light secretariat support.\n\nBring concrete proposals stating purpose, audience, owner, cost, funding source and demands on space and staff. Discuss them against the mission, local relevance, creative or learning value, feasibility and what they would displace.\n\n**Voting proposal for collective review:** Seek agreement before voting. Each advisory area has one vote, whether it has one or two members. Members sharing an area agree their position; if they cannot agree, that area abstains. A quorum requires at least five advisory areas represented, with at least four affirmative area votes for a council-level decision. Conflicted members declare their interest and step out. Confirm how vacancies, shared-area conflicts and recusals affect representation before adopting this procedure; if a valid quorum cannot be established, defer the decision. This procedure remains an open decision for the group.\n\nRecord decisions, reasons, owners and next actions. Routine programme delivery proceeds within delegated authority and does not await council votes.\n","council-option-2":"## Council structure\n\nA proposed 14-seat council brings together advisors, serving curators, community and youth representatives. The Director attends as operational lead.\n\nHeart and grace underpin how the council listens, collaborates and works with people.\n\nFour perspectives come together in one council. The groups describe contributions, not separate departments or ranks. Members can contribute beyond their category.\n\n| Group | Seats | Proposed roles and contribution |\n|---|---|---|\n| Practice & Systems Advisors | 5 | One each for Arts, Craft & Cultural Practice; Research, Learning & Publishing; Design, Systems & Technology; Business & Enterprise; Fundraising & Partnerships. Build capability, mentor the team and develop practitioner, institutional, industry and funding relationships. |\n| Programme members — ex officio | 5 | Festival Curator; Arts & New Media Curator; Sound & Music Curator; Film & Moving Image Curator; Craft & Material Practices Curator. Bring current practice, practitioner relationships and programme experience. |\n| Community representatives | 2 | Selected through a process developed with local residents, practitioners and participants. Bring priorities, access needs and lived experience; gather views and report back. |\n| Youth representatives | 2 | Young people selected through an accessible youth-led nomination or selection process. Help shape opportunities, learning and Kani’s future direction. |\n| **Total council seats** | **14** | The Director attends as operational lead, additional to these seats. Other working-team members join relevant items. |\n\nStart with one advisor per area. Invite further specialists for particular enquiries rather than automatically adding standing seats. Community and youth seats are distinct places; one person does not fill two seats.\n\n## Advisors and curators\n\nAn advisor supports the wider organisation across programmes and years. A curator develops a particular programme within an agreed brief. For example, the Arts, Craft & Cultural Practice Advisor helps build mentoring and institutional relationships; the Arts & New Media Curator develops the festival track.\n\nEx officio means membership by virtue of holding a role. Each serving curator joins through their appointment and leaves that seat when the appointment ends. These seats are additional to the advisors. An unfilled curatorial role leaves a vacant seat; no appointment is made solely to fill the council.\n\nAdvisors and community/youth representatives serve one-year terms, reviewed annually and renewable. Each has an agreed remit, time commitment and participation or compensation arrangement. Curatorial appointments include time for council work and handover.\n\nMembers step out of discussions concerning their own appointment, fees or performance. Interests in commissions, selections or partnerships are declared and handled through an agreed conflict process.\n\n## How the council works\n\nThe council helps shape annual priorities, participation and programme learning, and advises on partnerships and long-term development. The Director and programme leads handle everyday delivery within agreed briefs and budgets. Financial and contractual commitments remain with authorised entity representatives.\n\nOne Convenor is appointed annually from the council. The full council meets quarterly, with temporary working groups for specific questions. Advisors agree practical contributions such as mentoring, introductions or helping develop organisational systems.\n\nCommunity and youth representatives help set agendas, bring views from open community gatherings and report back on responses. Participation includes appropriate honoraria, travel and access support. Selection arrangements and decision procedures remain to be agreed.\n\nReview the council structure after one year.\n","council-option-3":"**Status: proposed model for discussion.** Membership, appointments, decision rules and financial delegations require collective agreement before adoption. The earlier governance and staffing chapters remain reference proposals; this chapter sets out Option 3 in full.\n\n## Executive summary\n\nKani Home is proposed as an organisation governed through **shared stewardship**. Authority is distributed across a council, a small working group and programme teams, with decisions made close to the work.\n\nA **16-member Shared Stewardship Council** brings together six advisors, three programme delegates, one Heart delegate, two working-team delegates, two community representatives and two youth representatives. It agrees direction, annual priorities, shared policies and resource-allocation principles, and reviews how Kani is working.\n\nA **Working Coordination Group** carries four responsibilities: Creative Practice & Learning; Community & Care; Partnerships & Institutional Development; and Systems, Operations & Resources. These are responsibilities, not four director posts. The group's size follows the actual appointments.\n\n**Year 1 centres on two continuing paid coordination roles: Programme Coordinator and Operations Coordinator.** Design & Communications works alongside them. Community & Care has allocated time within the team or part-time support; Partnerships can begin through a part-time or retained appointment. Existing accounts and hospitality staff support delivery. Each responsibility has a named owner, realistic capacity and appropriate compensation.\n\nProgramme teams make creative and delivery decisions within agreed briefs and budgets. Each programme has a named delivery owner. A **Council Convenor** maintains the governance process, with meeting facilitation shared or rotated and no casting vote or unilateral override.\n\nMajor direction is developed through consensus-seeking; practical, reviewable policies can use consent. Routine work proceeds through delegated authority. Heart underpins the whole structure through care, participation, respectful disagreement and responsibility towards people and place.\n\n## 1. How the structure works\n\n| Part | Purpose and authority | Boundary |\n|---|---|---|\n| Shared Stewardship Council | Direction, annual priorities, shared policies, resource-allocation principles and organisational review. | Does not approve every activity or give separate instructions to staff. |\n| Working Coordination Group | Connect programmes, community, partnerships and resources; coordinate capacity and resolve issues between teams. | Works within agreed priorities and delegated resources. |\n| Programme and working teams | Develop and deliver programmes and everyday services, with autonomy within their mandates. | Escalate matters affecting other teams, exceeding resources or changing agreed direction. |\n| Enabling entities | Hold the relevant institutional, financial and contractual responsibilities. | Reserved approvals and delegations must be explicit and aligned with actual entity mandates. |\n\nThe six advisors sit within the council as peers. Their advice alone is not an operational instruction. Every shared project still has one named person responsible for following it through.\n\n## 2. Council membership\n\n| Group | Seats | Contribution and selection |\n|---|---|---|\n| Practice advisors | 6 | Expertise, mentoring and organisational development; appointed against agreed criteria and needs. |\n| Programme delegates | 3 | One from each pillar, selected with the relevant programme teams. |\n| Heart delegate | 1 | Care, wellbeing and personal growth across Kani, including Heart programmes; selected with people involved in that work. |\n| Working-team delegates | 2 | Experience of running the place; selected by the working team. |\n| Community representatives | 2 | Local priorities, knowledge and access needs; selected through a process developed with participating communities. |\n| Youth representatives | 2 | Young people's perspectives on learning, participation and Kani's future; selected through an accessible youth-led process. |\n| **Total** | **16** | One person occupies one seat and has one decision-making position. |\n\nThe three programme delegates represent Research, Residencies & Learning; Creative Experiences; and Commercial Experiences & Space Hire. Their remit includes year-round activities beyond Kani Fest. Heart remains the foundation across the pillars, with a dedicated voice in governance.\n\nRepresentatives consult the people they represent and report back. Council seats are not additional staff posts. Invite specialists for particular questions without automatically adding standing seats.\n\n## 3. The six advisory roles\n\n| Advisory role | Contribution |\n|---|---|\n| Arts, Culture & Publishing | Creative practice, curatorial mentoring, publishing and cultural relationships. |\n| Research & Learning | Residencies, fellowships, learning approaches and research collaborations. |\n| Systems & Organisational Development | Governance, decision processes, team structures, documentation and organisational learning. |\n| Technology & Digital Practice | Creative technology, digital infrastructure, archives and technical capability. |\n| Business & Enterprise | Revenue models, pricing, commercial experiences, hospitality and sustainability. |\n| Institutional Partnerships & Fundraising | Institutional networks, grants, CSR, sponsorship and resource mobilisation. |\n\nEach advisor has an agreed contribution and time commitment. Advisors mentor, review and open relationships; named working-role holders carry the continuing work. For example, an advisor may introduce an art school, while the Partnerships Coordinator develops and maintains the collaboration.\n\n## 4. A light Working Coordination Group\n\nFour responsibilities are held across the people actually appointed. One person may hold more than one responsibility only where time, skills, support and workload make this realistic.\n\n| Responsibility | Suggested role title | Year 1 arrangement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Creative Practice & Learning | Programme Coordinator | Continuing paid role: programme development, creative quality, research, residencies, mentoring and connections across disciplines. |\n| Community & Care | Community & Care Coordinator | Defined responsibility with allocated paid time within the team, or part-time support: local relationships, access, participation, feedback and working culture. Works with Heart programme leads. |\n| Partnerships & Institutional Development | Partnerships Coordinator | Part-time or retained support: institutional relationships, funding development, proposals and follow-through, supported by relevant advisors. |\n| Systems, Operations & Resources | Operations Coordinator | Continuing paid role: shared calendar, resource planning, operational processes, documentation and coordination with accounts, hospitality and technical staff. |\n\n**The initial continuing team is Programme Coordinator, Operations Coordinator and Design & Communications.** Existing accounts and hospitality capacity is additional. Community & Care and Partnerships must have named owners and funded time; they are not assumed to happen through unpaid extra work. Additional support grows with programmes and funding.\n\nDesign & Communications leads graphics, audience communication and documentation coordination, working directly with programme owners. Curators, producers, editors, mentors, outreach support and technical specialists are appointed against programme needs and budgets. Systems coordination does not require the Operations Coordinator to personally supply every technical skill.\n\nThe Working Coordination Group consists of the people holding these ongoing responsibilities. Its membership need not be four people, and does not automatically expand when a project specialist is engaged. A named commercial booking owner works with Operations and Partnerships within agreed pricing and booking rules.\n\n### Authority and accountability\n\n- Each working role has a written mandate: decisions it can make, consultation requirements, resources, expected contributions and review dates.\n- The council appoints and reviews those holding the continuing coordination responsibilities through a defined process involving the working team. Initial role mandates run for one year, with a six-month review; employment or service terms are agreed separately.\n- Each person acts within their own mandate. Decisions affecting several functions involve the relevant colleagues and affected programme teams.\n- Each staff member has a named support and review contact, avoiding several competing managers.\n- Working Coordination Group members attend relevant council discussions. They hold a council decision-making seat only if selected into one of the existing 16 seats; there are no automatic extra seats or duplicate votes.\n- When responsibilities are combined in one person, required consultation or approval remains with a distinct authorised colleague. Combining roles must not become self-approval.\n\n## 5. Convening and facilitation\n\n| Role | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| Council Convenor | Gather agenda items, circulate information, organise meetings and follow up on governance commitments. |\n| Meeting facilitator | Guide a particular discussion, support equal participation and check agreement explicitly. |\n| Working-group Convenor | Organise coordination meetings and follow up on shared operational commitments; an assigned responsibility within the working group. |\n\nThe council selects its Convenor through nominations and a consent process for a one-year term, reviewed after six months. Meeting facilitation can rotate among prepared members or be supported externally. Initially, different people convene the council and the working group.\n\nThe Convenor has no casting vote, unilateral veto or power to change a collective decision. Every member can submit agenda items. The Convenor cannot exclude a concern without an agreed reason and route for consideration, and represents Kani's position only when authorised. Someone else facilitates matters involving the Convenor's own proposal or interests. Founders are eligible through defined roles; the position is not automatically theirs.\n\n## 6. Who decides what?\n\n| Decision | Decision owner | Required involvement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Purpose, ethos and major direction | Council | Wider consultation with teams and communities. |\n| Annual priorities | Council | Programme proposals and working-group capacity assessment. |\n| Annual programme allocations | Council | Accounts and relevant entity confirmation of funds and authorisations. |\n| Programme content and artistic selections | Relevant curator or programme team | Consultation appropriate to the agreed brief. |\n| Routine spending within an allocation | Authorised budget holder | Agreed financial process and limits. |\n| Shared-space or staffing clashes | Affected teams first; working group if unresolved | People responsible for affected work. |\n| Routine institutional collaboration | Partnerships Coordinator within delegated authority | Relevant programme and resource leads. |\n| Major partnership or long-term commitment | Council and relevant entity | Assessment of value, capacity and obligations. |\n| Operational systems and procedures | Relevant function, or working group for shared policies | Staff and users affected by the change. |\n| Appointment and review of coordination-role holders | Council | Working-team input; conflicted members step aside. |\n| Programme specialist appointments | Relevant team under an agreed process | Budget holder and required entity authorisation. |\n\nEvery project has a named delivery owner, including work spanning pillars. Within its agreed brief and resources, a team can proceed without repeated council approval. Advisors, founders and funders use these arrangements rather than giving parallel instructions to staff.\n\n## 7. Reaching agreement\n\n| Decision type | Method |\n|---|---|\n| Routine work within an agreed mandate | Responsible person decides, consulting affected people. |\n| Practical policies and reversible experiments | Consent, with limits and a review date. |\n| Major organisational direction | Consensus-seeking, with wider consultation. |\n| Immediate operational emergency | Defined emergency authority, followed by review. |\n\n**Consensus** develops a proposal members can support or live with; it need not be everyone's first preference. **Consent** asks whether there is a reasoned objection to trying a proposal within defined limits. It is useful for decisions that can be reviewed and changed. Both require understanding, meaningful participation and an explicit check.\n\n### The decision process\n\n1. **Prepare:** circulate the need, proposal, costs, affected people, decision owner and review date.\n2. **Clarify:** answer questions before debating positions.\n3. **Hear:** give each eligible participant an opportunity to contribute, with language and access support where needed.\n4. **Revise:** address concerns and consider alternatives.\n5. **Check:** ask each eligible participant to state their position explicitly.\n6. **Record:** document the decision, reservations, owner, resources and next steps.\n\n| Position | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Support | I support the proposal proceeding. |\n| Accept with reservations | I can live with it; record my concern. |\n| Stand aside | I do not support it, but will not prevent others proceeding; implications for delivery must be clarified. |\n| Object | I see a significant problem concerning consequences, feasibility or alignment with Kani's purpose that needs resolving. |\n\nFacilitators help people explain concerns without requiring technical or polished language. **Silence and unanswered messages do not count as agreement.** Standing aside does not imply agreement to provide labour or resources.\n\n### When agreement is not reached\n\nIdentify whether the issue is missing information, competing needs, resources, values or a damaged working relationship. Revise the proposal, develop alternatives with those raising concerns, seek independent facilitation, agree a smaller trial, or defer or withdraw it. A deadline does not automatically transfer authority to the Convenor.\n\nA possible voting fallback is approval by three-quarters of eligible council members after a second facilitated discussion, limited to specified decisions. This is a proposal, not an adopted rule: eligible membership, rounding, recusals and the permitted decisions must be defined before use. A result reached this way is recorded as a supermajority decision, not consensus. Until a fallback is agreed, an unresolved proposal remains unresolved.\n\nFor a fully occupied 16-seat council, the proposed quorum is **11**, including at least two community/youth representatives and two programme, Heart or working-team delegates. Vacancy, recusal, written-participation and agreement-check rules must be settled before adoption; the six advisors cannot decide alone.\n\n## 8. Budgets, spaces and shared resources\n\nEach programme agreement identifies its purpose, scope, owner, budget, funding conditions, staff time, spaces, equipment, spending authority, participation responsibilities and review dates. Accounts provides an accessible picture of available, committed and spent funds.\n\n| Question | Proposed arrangement |\n|---|---|\n| Who allocates resources across programmes? | Council, informed by actual funding and team capacity. |\n| Who spends within an allocation? | Named authorised budget holder, within written limits. |\n| Who agrees transfers between programmes? | Working group within an agreed limit, with affected budget holders; council beyond it, with required entity authorisation. |\n| How are commercial and public activities balanced? | Shared calendar and allocation policy, including protected time for public-interest work. |\n| How are equipment and spaces maintained? | Transparent booking rules, named maintenance responsibilities and a process for clashes. |\n\nCommercial bookings account for their full demands on staff and facilities. Kani can consider a defined programme fund whose allocations involve community and youth representatives. Institutional relationships, agreements and commitments are documented so continuity does not depend on one person.\n\n## 9. Founders and enabling entities\n\nThe proposed roles of SoUF, KiLab and M/s Kani Home must be aligned with their actual mandates and authorisations. A written delegation agreement identifies lead entities, signatories, expenditure powers, team authority, reserved matters and a route for disagreements.\n\nFounders participate through defined responsibilities; any retained approval powers are stated openly. Council agreement alone does not authorise a financial or contractual commitment. Delegations must nevertheless give teams enough authority to carry out approved work without repeatedly seeking fresh permission.\n\n## 10. Participation, care and conflicts of interest\n\nCouncil appointments run for one year, reviewed annually, with staggered handovers where possible. Agree selection criteria, nomination routes, expected contributions, participation support and a fair replacement process. Community and youth representatives need accessible ways to gather views and report back.\n\nBudget for appropriate honoraria, travel, language and access support. Declare interests in suppliers, applicants, partners and commissions. Members step aside from decisions about their own appointment, fees or connected interests. A replacement or conduct process includes an opportunity to respond.\n\nHeart shapes everyday practice. Every lead remains responsible for respectful working and realistic workloads. Confidential concerns have an impartial route and an alternative contact when the usual lead, founder or Convenor is involved. Sensitive complaints are handled separately from public consensus discussions.\n\nDefine emergency authority before launch: who can act, for what situations, within what limits and how promptly decisions are reported and reviewed. Emergency authority is not a general route around agreed governance.\n\n## 11. Working rhythm and records\n\n| Gathering | Proposed rhythm | Focus |\n|---|---|---|\n| Programme-team check-ins | According to the work | Delivery and immediate decisions. |\n| Working Coordination Group | Fortnightly; weekly during busy periods | Shared capacity, resources and issues between teams. |\n| Council | Quarterly, with additional decision meetings when needed | Direction, budgets, policy and accountability. |\n| Community gathering | Twice yearly | Priorities, feedback and reporting back. |\n| Governance review | After six months, then annually | Participation, authority, workload and effectiveness. |\n\nThe shared decision record captures what was agreed, why, who participated, who will act and when it will be reviewed. Confidential personnel and complaint records have separate restricted access. Feedback receives a response: what changes, what needs exploration and what cannot proceed, with reasons.\n\nReviews ask whose contributions shape decisions, who controls information and resources, whether staff can disagree, whether community and youth members influence outcomes, and whether coordination work is recognised and paid.\n\n## 12. Example: an institutional residency\n\n1. The Partnerships Coordinator develops an opportunity with an art or design school.\n2. The Programme Coordinator and relevant practitioners develop the brief.\n3. The person responsible for Community & Care helps shape local participation, access and support.\n4. The Operations Coordinator assesses budget, spaces, staffing and calendar implications.\n5. The proposal proceeds within existing delegation, or goes to the council if it introduces a major commitment or changes priorities.\n6. The relevant entity authorises the agreement.\n7. A named residency lead delivers within the agreed mandate.\n8. Participants and partners contribute to a review informing future work.\n\nWhere someone holds two functions, these remain distinct checks and consultation involves the affected colleagues. The council sets direction and boundaries, the working functions make collaboration possible, and the programme team carries the work.\n\n## 13. Starting the model\n\nBegin with a six-month pilot after agreeing:\n\n- Founder and entity delegations, financial limits and reserved matters.\n- Council selection, representative mandates, participation support and handovers.\n- Paid time, staffing and authority for the four coordination responsibilities, with the lean Year 1 arrangements confirmed.\n- Quorum, consent and consensus procedures, vacancies, recusals and any voting fallback.\n- Convenor selection, independent routes for concerns and emergency arrangements.\n- A manageable programme calendar and a review process.\n\nTest the arrangements against a Kani Fest budget, an institutional residency partnership and a commercial booking that clashes with a community programme. The pilot reviews whether authority is shared in practice, decisions sit at the right level, workload is manageable and responsibilities are fulfilled.\n\n## References informing the discussion\n\nThese sources offer practices to learn from; Option 3 is a proposed adaptation for Kani, not a claim to reproduce any one organisation's governance.\n\n- [Kalpavriksh: Philosophy and Working Principles](https://kalpavriksh.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/KV-philosophy-note-revised-Oct-2019-clean.pdf) — collective direction and project responsibility.\n- [Seeds for Change: Consensus Decision Making](https://www.seedsforchange.org.uk/consensus) — participation, reservations, objections and facilitation.\n- [Sociocracy For All: Consent Decision Making](https://www.sociocracyforall.org/consent-decision-making/) — consent and reasoned objections.\n- [Casa do Povo](https://casadopovo.org.br/about/) — participatory cultural space and differentiated institutional roles.\n- [UHAI: participatory grantmaking case study](https://icscentre.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/UHAI_ThePowerofMakingFutures.pdf) — community involvement in resource allocation.\n- [ruangrupa and lumbung](https://documenta-fifteen.de/en/about/) — collective resource sharing.\n","decisions":"**Reference draft for Option 1.** [Option 2](#council-option-2) sets out a participatory council. [Option 3](#council-option-3) contains its own shared-stewardship decision rights, lean staffing, consensus and consent procedures. The Director-based rights below are retained as an earlier proposal; they do not govern Option 3.\n\n| Decision | Responsible role |\n|---|---|\n| Overall direction and annual priorities | Council collectively, informed by the Director and working team. |\n| Year-round programme, staff allocation and operational commitments | Director of Creative Practice, within delegated authority. |\n| Festival artistic selections | Festival Curator, informed by track curators/fellows, within the agreed brief and budget. |\n| Festival programme and resource review | Festival Curator and Producer jointly review the programme; the Curator owns artistic choices and the Producer owns delivery planning. Escalate unresolved resource conflicts to the Director. |\n| Festival confirmed timetable | Programme Coordinator maintains the schedule and change log after review by the relevant leads. |\n| Festival equipment allocation and changeovers | Technical & Infrastructure Lead under the Producer, working with venue/session leads and shared technical staff. |\n| Festival volunteer coordination | Volunteer Coordinator owns induction, rota and support; one assigned venue or task lead directs each shift. |\n| Publication editorial development and quality | Editor / Publishing Lead, within the agreed publishing brief and budget. |\n| Marketplace theme and participant selection | Marketplace Curator, within the agreed programme brief. |\n| Outreach strategy and key institutional relationships | Director, informed by programme owners and community feedback. |\n| Community relationships, participation support and follow-up | Programme & Operations Coordinator in Year 1; dedicated outreach coordinator when appointed, working with the named programme owner. |\n| Audience communication and development | Design & Communications, alongside programme owners and outreach coordination. |\n| Project schedules, participant coordination and delivery follow-up | Named programme manager or coordinator. |\n| Technical feasibility, venue readiness and production methods | Operations / Production Lead or the coordinator with appointed technical specialists. |\n| Funding proposals and funder relationships | Fundraising lead/manager with Director, programme and accounts inputs. |\n| Commercial proposals and bookings | Named commercial owner within approved pricing and booking rules. |\n| Team care, morale and working culture | Director accountable; named Heart & Wellness coordinator supports check-ins and practical care. Every lead remains responsible for their team’s working conditions. |\n| Contracts and financial commitments | Authorised representatives of the relevant entity. |\n\nWhen scope exceeds agreed resources, the relevant creative lead and Director revise the proposal or seek additional support. Council endorsement alone does not authorise spending.\n\nEvery project has one named owner and a clear budget, including when it connects pillars or platforms. For shared projects, agree who owns each deliverable and how budgets and handovers are coordinated.\n\nThe council’s proposed voting method gives one vote to each advisory area, regardless of whether it has one or two members. It remains subject to collective agreement; see [Council appointments and decision procedure](#council) and [Open Decisions](#open-decisions).\n","year-one":"**Earlier staffing reference:** This chapter describes the Director-based proposal. [Council Option 3](#council-option-3) sets out the alternative with Programme and Operations Coordinators, Design & Communications, and allocated or part-time support for Community & Care and Partnerships. These are alternative models for discussion, not cumulative staffing commitments.\n\nBegin with a small permanent team, supported by existing staff and funded specialist appointments. Add continuing roles as programmes, workload and resources grow.\n\nStart with **three permanent hires**, supported by the existing accounts and hospitality teams and a defined budget for retained and project expertise.\n\n## Director of Creative Practice\n\nThe single operational head of the working team.\n\n- Leads the year-round programme across the three pillars.\n\n- Is accountable for outreach and community engagement, supporting key relationships and reviewing participation and access.\n\n- Works with the council on priorities, partnerships and institutional development.\n\n- Holds the overall calendar, resource plan and programme budget within delegated authority.\n\n- Commissions curators, editors, mentors and other specialists.\n\n- Leads key fundraising relationships with retained support.\n\n- Develops and reviews platforms and original initiatives.\n\n## Roles reporting to the Director\n\n| Role | Initial responsibilities |\n|---|---|\n| Programme & Operations Coordinator | Shared calendar, resident and artist liaison, local community relationships, participation support and follow-up; production logistics, vendor coordination, commercial enquiries and booking follow-up; coordination with accounts and hospitality. |\n| Design & Communications Associate | Graphics, social media, programme announcements, audience communication and development with programme owners, presentations and documentation coordination. An early-career role with independent working ability. |\n| Existing Accounts Team | Invoicing, payments, records, expenditure tracking and financial reporting inputs. Entity approval powers remain separate. |\n| Existing Hospitality Team | Accommodation, guest arrivals, room readiness, catering arrangements, routine venue visits and visitor support. |\n\nThe Director is one of the three new permanent hires. Existing accounts and hospitality capacity is additional to those three hires.\n\n## Retained, rotating and project basis\n\n| Support role | Starting arrangement |\n|---|---|\n| Fundraising & Partnerships Lead | Part-time retainer with defined deliverables, reporting to the Director. Develops grants, CSR opportunities, sponsors, donors and institutional relationships. |\n| Proposal & Grant Writer | Commissioned for selected applications and reports; coordinated by the fundraising lead. Can be the same person where skills and capacity allow. |\n| AV Technician | Scheduled event support and maintenance visits. Train an existing staff member for basic playback and equipment checks. |\n| Student Design Intern | Paid, supervised placement under the Design & Communications Associate, with defined learning outcomes. |\n| Senior Design Mentor | Occasional support for visual direction, review and development of the associate. |\n| Festival Curator, curatorial fellows & Festival Producer | Edition-funded appointments; proposed 6–9 month paid track fellowships with mentoring and production support. |\n| Festival delivery roles | Programme Coordinator, Technical & Infrastructure Lead, venue/session leads, Artist & Guest Liaison and Volunteer Coordinator; combine roles only where capacity allows. |\n| Editor / Publishing Lead | Retained or appointed per publication or series, supported by commissioned editorial specialists. |\n| Marketplace Curator | Contract across a series of editions, supported by the permanent coordinator. |\n| Heart programme leads, mentors & facilitators | Funded appointments for initiatives such as Nurupa; define delivery time, participant support and specialist requirements before launch. |\n| Residency mentors & facilitators | Appointments against each programme’s budget, including researchers, artists, educators, creative technologists and local knowledge holders. |\n| Specialist production partners | Sound, lighting, fabrication, installation, photography, video and event crew as required. |\n\nStart with a manageable calendar. A festival or publication deadline must carry its additional staffing cost rather than simply being added to the coordinator’s existing workload.\n\n## Outreach capacity\n\nThe coordinator maintains the shared relationship record and follows up on programme feedback, while the Director supports key relationships. Allocate time for this work and fund additional outreach support during busy programmes. There is no additional permanent outreach hire in the initial three-person team. See [Outreach & Community Engagement](#outreach).\n\nKani Fest budgets for its own delivery capacity and supported volunteer teams. The permanent coordinator holds continuity; returning volunteers can take bounded team roles, while sustained responsibilities require a funded appointment. See the [festival working map](#kani-fest).\n\n## Heart & Wellness\n\nName a coordinator for team welcome, check-ins, morale, recognition and practical support, with time allocated within the starting structure. The Director is accountable for making this possible; every lead owns respectful working and realistic workloads. At festival peaks, agree additional support alongside the Producer and Volunteer Coordinator. Provide an alternative contact for concerns involving the usual lead.\n\n[Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness) also holds programmes for growth, wellbeing and becoming, including Nurupa in development. Each initiative needs a defined brief, named lead and funded delivery capacity; detailed arrangements remain open.\n\n","full-structure":"**Earlier staffing reference:** This chapter describes the Director-based proposal. [Council Option 3](#council-option-3) sets out the alternative with Programme and Operations Coordinators, Design & Communications, and allocated or part-time support for Community & Care and Partnerships. These are alternative models for discussion, not cumulative staffing commitments.\n\n| Area | Role | Core responsibility |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Programme management | Programme Manager — Research, Residencies & Learning | Owns residencies, fellowships, research, educational relationships and participant support. |\n| Programme management | Programme Manager — Creative Experiences | Owns the public calendar and delivery coordination for exhibitions, music, film, workshops, walks, marketplaces and Kani Fest. |\n| Community engagement | Community Engagement & Outreach Coordinator | Maintains local relationships, coordinates participation and access, supports programme outreach and follows up on feedback across the pillars. |\n| Fundraising, partnerships and commercial development | Fundraising & Partnerships Manager | Owns the funding strategy and pipeline, funder relationships, grants, CSR partnerships, sponsorships, donors and institutional collaborations. |\n| Fundraising, partnerships and commercial development | Commercial & Bookings Executive | Manages enquiries, proposals, quotations, site visits, follow-up, confirmed bookings and client relationships. |\n| Fundraising, partnerships and commercial development | Proposal & Grant Writer | Develops applications, concept notes, CSR and sponsorship proposals, pitch material and narrative reports. |\n| Operations and technical delivery | Operations / Production Lead | Owns venue readiness, the master venue calendar, logistics, maintenance coordination, vendors and on-site delivery. |\n| Operations and technical delivery | AV & Technical Operator | Operates sound, projection and screening systems, maintains equipment and coordinates with specialist vendors. |\n| Design and communications | Design & Communications Lead | Owns visual communication, social media planning, audience outreach, announcements, newsletters, presentations and signage across all programmes. |\n| Design and communications | Student Design Intern | Supports production through a supervised rotating placement. |\n| Existing support teams | Accounts Team | Supports project owners and fundraising staff while retaining entity-specific approval procedures. |\n| Existing support teams | Hospitality Team | Works with production, programme and bookings staff on delivery. |\n\nAll continuing roles sit within one permanent working team led by the **Director of Creative Practice**. This is a growth model; appointments follow recurring need and funding rather than being simultaneous Year 1 hires.\n\n## Programme management\n\n- **Programme Manager — Research, Residencies & Learning:** Owns residencies, fellowships, research, educational relationships and participant support. Shapes the research and learning pipeline, from developing opportunities and selecting participants to coordinating mentors, partners and outcomes. Ensures participants receive clear support and that learning is documented and shared.\n\n- **Programme Manager — Creative Experiences:** Owns the public calendar and coordinates delivery for exhibitions, music, film, workshops, walks, marketplaces and Kani Fest. Works with the Programme Manager — Research, Residencies & Learning when projects produce public events, ensuring that each project has one named owner and clear delivery responsibilities.\n\nBoth report to the Director. Where a residency produces a public event, the two managers agree the handover and responsibilities for each part.\n\n## Community engagement and outreach\n\n**Community Engagement & Outreach Coordinator — reports to the Director**\n\nMaintains local and community relationships across programmes, coordinates participation and access support, keeps the shared relationship record and follows up on feedback. Works with programme managers on co-development and with Design & Communications on audience development. Programme owners retain responsibility for creative briefs, budgets, selections and commitments.\n\nIn Year 1 these duties are allocated to the Programme & Operations Coordinator with Director support. Establish the dedicated role when recurring relationships and participation work exceed that capacity and funding can sustain it. See [Outreach & Community Engagement](#outreach).\n\n## Fundraising, partnerships and commercial development\n\n**Fundraising & Partnerships Manager — reports to the Director**\n\nThis operational role works alongside the Advisory & Programme Council’s **Fundraising & Resource Development** advisory role. The advisor provides strategic guidance and relationships; the Manager owns day-to-day fundraising delivery, partnerships and reporting.\n\nOwns the funding strategy and pipeline, funder relationships, grants, CSR partnerships, sponsorships, donors and institutional collaborations. Oversees commercial development and coordinates funder reporting.\n\nReporting to this manager:\n\n- **Commercial & Bookings Executive:** Enquiries, proposals, quotations, site visits, follow-up, confirmed bookings and client relationships.\n\n- **Proposal & Grant Writer:** Applications, concept notes, CSR and sponsorship proposals, pitch material and narrative reports. Can remain external or retained.\n\nProgramme managers design and deliver funded activities and collect outcome evidence. Accounts supplies expenditure records and financial reporting. The Fundraising Manager coordinates the combined report and funder relationship.\n\n## Operations and technical delivery\n\n**Operations / Production Lead — reports to the Director**\n\nOwns venue readiness, the master venue calendar, logistics, maintenance coordination, vendors and on-site delivery.\n\n- **AV & Technical Operator — reports to the Operations Lead:** Operates sound, projection and screening systems, maintains equipment and coordinates with specialist vendors.\n\n- External production partners provide additional capacity per project.\n\n## Design and communications\n\n**Design & Communications Lead — reports to the Director**\n\nOwns visual communication, social media planning, audience outreach, announcements, newsletters, presentations and signage across all programmes.\n\n- **Student Design Intern — reports to the Design Lead:** Supports production through a supervised rotating placement.\n\n- Programme owners provide accurate information; the communications lead coordinates public-facing editing and approval.\n\n- Editors own publication manuscripts and editorial quality. Publication design is planned with the design team.\n\n- Photography, recording and editing receive allocated time or commissioned support.\n\n## Existing support teams\n\n- **Accounts Team:** Operationally coordinated under the Director; works with project owners and fundraising staff while retaining entity-specific approval procedures.\n\n- **Hospitality Team:** Operationally coordinated under the Director; works with production, programme and bookings staff on delivery.\n\n## Kani Fest project team\n\nThe Festival Curator and Festival Producer work alongside one another under the Director. Proposed 6–9 month paid curatorial fellows develop the tracks; the Producer coordinates programme scheduling, technical infrastructure, venue/session leads, artist liaison and volunteer coordination. These are edition appointments or allocated responsibilities, not automatic additions to the permanent team. The permanent coordinator in Year 1, or Creative Experiences Programme Manager at scale, holds continuity between editions. See the [festival working map and role explainers](#kani-fest).\n\n## Heart & Wellness\n\nA shared function with a named coordinator accountable to the Director, supporting staff, fellows, project teams and volunteers across Kani. It coordinates welcome, check-ins, recognition, listening and practical care. It can be allocated within an existing role initially; dedicated capacity follows workload and funding. All team leads retain responsibility for respectful working, realistic workloads and follow-through on concerns. See [Shared Infrastructure](#infrastructure).\n\n[Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness) also holds programmes for growth, wellbeing and becoming, including Nurupa in development. Each initiative needs a defined brief, named lead and funded delivery capacity; detailed arrangements remain open.\n\n","growth":"**Earlier staffing reference:** This chapter describes the Director-based proposal. [Council Option 3](#council-option-3) sets out the alternative with Programme and Operations Coordinators, Design & Communications, and allocated or part-time support for Community & Care and Partnerships. These are alternative models for discussion, not cumulative staffing commitments.\n\nGrowth follows recurring work and funding rather than a fixed hiring sequence.\n\n| Trigger | Staffing response |\n|---|---|\n| Credible funding opportunities require sustained follow-up. | Make Fundraising & Partnerships Manager permanent. |\n| Residencies, public programmes and commercial hires regularly overlap. | Separate programme management from operations/production coordination. |\n| Community relationships, participation support and follow-up become sustained work beyond the programme coordinator’s capacity. | Add a Community Engagement & Outreach Coordinator reporting to the Director, with recurring funding and a defined cross-programme remit. |\n| Technical activity becomes frequent and predictable. | Bring in a permanent AV & Technical Operator. |\n| Commercial enquiries and bookings become a substantial recurring business. | Add a Commercial & Bookings Executive under Fundraising & Partnerships. |\n| Research and public programmes each sustain a funded calendar. | Create dedicated managers for Research, Residencies & Learning and Creative Experiences. |\n| Publication work becomes a sustained, funded annual programme. | Expand the editor’s retained time; consider a continuing publishing role only when justified. |\n| Festival or marketplace peaks exceed normal capacity. | Add temporary producers, coordinators and crew through the relevant programme budget. |\n\nBefore each permanent addition, confirm recurring need, a clear reporting line and visibility of funding for the role’s full cost over the following 12 months. This is a proposed planning rule, not a commitment to hire.\n\nFor Kani Fest, returning volunteers may take defined team-captain responsibilities with training and staff support. Sustained work across the year should become a scoped, funded associate or fellowship opportunity where justified. This is an optional pathway based on interest, readiness and available roles, not a guaranteed promotion. See [Kani Fest](#kani-fest).\n\nReview capacity for Heart & Wellness as staff, fellows, volunteers and project teams grow. Begin with a named coordinator and allocated time; add dedicated support when recurring need and funding justify it.\n\n[Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness) also holds programmes for growth, wellbeing and becoming, including Nurupa in development. Each initiative needs a defined brief, named lead and funded delivery capacity; detailed arrangements remain open.\n\n"}};
